/* Exam 3 "Hear the why": spoken explanations for the L13–L15 questions (Dr. Smith). Loaded after narration.js. */
Object.assign(NARR_Q, {
"L13-001": "Coral snake venom is a toxin, and the fumes from a factory smokestack are a toxicant. Both words name noxious substances, but they differ by origin. A toxin comes from a biological system, so snake venom, made by the snake, is a toxin. A toxicant is made by man through human activity, so whatever is harmful in the smokestack emission is a toxicant. In class she said snake venom could be called poisonous, but technically it is a toxin. Swapping the two reverses the origins. Calling both of them toxins ignores that the smokestack fumes come from human activity. Poison and xenobiotic fit both substances loosely. Poison is the general lay term for anything that can cause a bad response, and a xenobiotic is any compound the organism does not normally make or expect, so neither word says where the substance came from. She added that she will often not use these two words properly herself, but they are different words.",
"L13-002": "A xenobiotic is a compound that is not normally produced by the organism or expected to be present in it. That is the whole definition. It says nothing about where the compound came from and nothing about harm. So a xenobiotic can be a snake toxin, a man-made toxicant, a food additive or a medication. In class she said it just means any compound that gets into an organism that the organism does not normally make or expect to be there. The other three statements are the other definitions. A toxic substance made by a biological system is a toxin, such as snake venom. A toxic substance made by human activity is a toxicant, such as a smokestack emission. Any agent that causes a deleterious response is a poison. Toxin and toxicant describe origin, and poison describes harm. Xenobiotic describes only being foreign.",
"L13-003": "Three of the choices are principles of toxicology: exposure, A D M E T, and target and mechanism of action. The full list of five is source, exposure, A D M E T, dose, and target and mechanism of action. Each principle asks one question. Source asks where the poison comes from. Exposure asks how people or critters are exposed, through route, duration, single versus repeated exposure, and frequency. A D M E T is absorption, distribution, metabolism and excretion, with a T added for toxicity, and its metabolism step includes bioactivation. Dose asks whether the exposure is high enough to cause a problem. Target and mechanism of action asks where the substance goes in the body and how it causes harm. Route of exposure is wrong because it is one factor inside the exposure principle, not a principle of its own. The therapeutic index is wrong because it is a value calculated from dose-response curves within the dose principle.",
"L13-004": "The T added to A D M E stands for toxicity. A D M E is absorption, distribution, metabolism and excretion, the kinetics of anything that gets into the body: it is absorbed or injected, distributed, likely metabolized, then excreted. In class she said that because this is the toxicology module, the T is added, so the principle reads absorption, distribution, metabolism, elimination, and toxicity. The question toxicologists add is whether those kinetics end in harm. The step they watch most is metabolism, because metabolism can turn a compound into something even nastier, which she calls bioactivation. Target is the tempting choice, but target and mechanism of action is a separate principle of its own, not a letter in A D M E T. Threshold belongs to the dose principle and the dose-response curve. Transport is wrong because the added letter names the outcome toxicologists care about, toxicity, not another kinetic step.",
"L13-005": "The correct statement is that the toxic target may differ from the therapeutic target. This is the target and mechanism of action principle, which asks where a substance goes in the body and how it causes harm. Acetaminophen is her example. Her husband took it for soreness, but its toxicity comes from N A P Q I, a metabolite made by cytochrome P four fifty two E one, and N A P Q I is toxic to the liver. So the harm happens at a different target from the benefit, and metabolism made the toxic species. That rules out the statement that metabolism does not contribute to the toxic effect. A substance can also have several targets, with a different mechanism at each, so the mechanism is not the same at every target. In class she said that sometimes the target of something toxic is not the same target as its therapeutic target. The dose that reaches each target decides whether toxicity happens there.",
"L13-006": "Mixing chemotherapy in a hood with the wrong airflow is an occupational exposure. Occupational exposures are workplace exposures, and healthcare workers have them too. Her healthcare examples were chemotherapy mixed in an incorrect hood, radiation, and a patient who arrives in the emergency room splattered with an organophosphate, whose contaminant can expose the staff. The source category follows the setting of the exposure, not the substance. Chemotherapy is a medication, but the medications category means a patient receiving the drug, and here the pharmacist is exposed through the job. Environmental exposure is wrong because it means pollution in the surroundings, such as diesel fumes from a passing bus or dust blowing from a landfill. Critter bites and stings are wrong because they cover venoms from animals such as snakes, and no animal is involved here. The four source categories are environmental, occupational, critter bites and stings, and medications. In this course, occupational exposure means chemicals, not pathogens such as needle sticks.",
"L13-007": "From most to least effective, the routes are intravenous, inhalation, oral, then dermal. In class she said that if a compound is a liquid that can be injected, intravenous is the most effective route, and that inhalation, oral and dermal decrease in effectiveness after it. Her route example shows one reason oral sits below intravenous: an oral dose goes to the liver first and undergoes first-pass effect, while an intravenous dose does not. Two rankings are easy to mix up. Oral is the most common route of exposure, followed by inhalation, topical and parenteral, but most common is not most effective, so the order that starts with oral is wrong. The order that starts with inhalation is wrong because intravenous ranks first and dermal last. The order that starts with dermal reads the list backward, from least to most effective. Healthcare sees more parenteral exposure than other settings, through injected medications and drug abuse.",
"L13-008": "At equal doses, the oral dose is less toxic than the intravenous dose. An oral dose goes to the liver first and undergoes first-pass effect. For this compound the liver makes a less toxic product, so part of the oral dose is made safer before it reaches the rest of the body. An intravenous dose does not go through first-pass effect, so the whole dose reaches the body in its more toxic form. In class she called the oral route the least scary one here, because first pass helps protect you, metabolizing and clearing the compound faster than if it were just given by intravenous injection. The statement that the intravenous dose is less toxic is reversed. The statement that the oral dose skips first-pass metabolism is also reversed, because the intravenous dose is the one that skips it. The two doses are not equally toxic, because route changes toxicity whenever first-pass metabolism changes the compound.",
"L13-009": "The correct statement is that extended-release tablets can clump in the stomach and then release the drug at once. Asked whether a handful of immediate-release or a handful of extended-release beta blockers is worse, she answered that it depends. A handful of immediate-release tablets is absorbed pretty quickly. Extended-release tablets often clump together in the stomach, and then all of a sudden the drug is released. Later in the course she called this clump a bezoar and said that if it breaks apart, a huge amount of drug is absorbed at once, which is why a patient found within that window needs to go to the hospital. So extended-release tablets do not release the drug faster than immediate release. The formulation does change the toxicity of an overdose, and so does the vehicle, for example an oily vehicle compared with an aqueous one, so the statement that the vehicle and not the formulation decides is wrong. She added that the formulation really affects how the poisoned patient is treated.",
"L13-010": "Acute is less than twenty four hours. Subacute is less than one month. Subchronic is one to three months. Chronic is more than three months, up to years. These are the defined durations of exposure for animal studies, from the classic Casarett and Doull toxicology textbook. Because the animals are dosed for a set length of time, the study knows how long they were exposed and can look at the effects of that exposure. Humans can be exposed both acutely and chronically, but a person often does not realize an exposure is happening or cannot remember when it began. Toxicologists still try to fit human exposures into these categories, to relate the person's response to the duration. The pairs most easily swapped are subacute and subchronic. Under a month is subacute, and one to three months is subchronic. Chronic begins only after three months.",
"L13-011": "The benzene example shows that a single exposure and a repeated exposure can differ in effect. Benzene is a gas that workers cannot see or smell. One walk into the building gives a headache and sleepiness. Going in every day for years is chronic low-dose exposure, and benzene is a carcinogen that causes leukemia. So the effect of one exposure does not predict the effect of repeated exposure. In class she said that getting sleepy and a headache is really different from getting leukemia. Tolerance is the tempting answer, but the daily worker still gets the headache every day, so no tolerance develops. The route is the same in both cases, breathing the gas. Leukemia is not an immediate effect. It is the delayed effect of the chronic exposure.",
"L13-012": "The daughters' cancer is a delayed effect of an exposure. Diethylstilbestrol, called D E S, is an estrogen that was given in the nineteen fifties to help women keep a pregnancy. The mothers had little more than nausea, which was the immediate effect. Their daughters were exposed in the womb, grew up, and about twenty years later developed a rare vaginal cancer. Because the harm appeared decades later and in a different person, it took epidemiology to link it to D E S. In class she called it a very weird and scary phenomenon about twenty years after the women were given D E S. The immediate effect was the nausea, not the cancer. Tolerance is about a repeated exposure losing its effect, which is not what happened here. Hormesis is a protective effect at a low dose of a substance the body does not need, and this effect was harmful.",
"L13-013": "The daily headache shows that a chronic exposure can give an immediate effect after each exposure. The leak is never fixed, so each day's walk into the building is a new exposure, and each one brings the same headache and sleepiness. The worker develops no tolerance. In class she said that chronic exposure may produce immediate effects after each exposure, and that the workers are not developing any tolerance. So a chronic exposure can carry both kinds of effect: the immediate one after every exposure, and for benzene the delayed risk of leukemia. That rules out the statement that chronic exposure gives only delayed effects. Tolerance did not develop, because the headache returns every day. Build-up in the blood is the point of the frequency figure, not of this example. Here each day is a separate exposure with its own immediate effect.",
"L13-014": "Compound A has the longest half-life. Half-life is the time it takes for half of the compound to be cleared from the blood. After a single dose, a flatter fall in blood concentration means a longer half-life. In the figure, A barely slopes down, with a half-life of about one year. B falls faster, with a half-life of about one day, about equal to the dosing interval. C drops steeply, with a half-life of about five hours, because it is metabolized and cleared quickly. In class she said that A has the longest half-life because it takes longer for half the compound to be cleared out of the blood, and that C has the shortest. A and B are not equal. A year and a day are very different, and the flatter line belongs to A.",
"L13-015": "Compound C never reaches the toxic concentration, because its short half-life clears each dose before the next one arrives. In the repeated-dose panel, each dose is one quarter of the single dose, so four doses add up to the single dose. A concentration above two at the target site is toxic for all three compounds. A compound accumulates when its half-life is long compared with the time between doses. A, with the longest half-life, builds up into the toxic zone by about the third dose. B builds up more slowly and is in the toxic zone by about the fourth dose, and for sure by the fifth. C, which she said has a really short half-life and clears really quickly, never gets into the toxic zone at that dose and frequency. So A alone, B alone, and B and C together are all wrong, because A and B both reach the toxic zone. For a medication, the dosing frequency would be stretched out so the concentration stays below toxicity but high enough to work, like C.",
"L13-016": "Four choices are correct: an allergic reaction, toxicity at a different target site, synergy with another drug, and damage at the injection site. Only accumulation into the toxic range is wrong. C does not build up, because its short half-life clears each dose before the next; A and B are the compounds that accumulate. Her twist is that C can still cause a bad response in ways that have nothing to do with kinetics. An allergy would not take very much drug, so it does not depend on the blood concentration. The curve shows one particular target, and C could go to a different target site and cause toxicity there. C could synergize with other drugs, and she said you could end up with additive adverse effects. A drug injected subcutaneously or intradermally can give a therapeutic response yet damage the injection site, which she called an adverse response. She said C is not necessarily all good to go, and healthcare providers have to watch for these problems.",
"L13-017": "Metabolism that turns a compound into something more harmful than the parent is bioactivation. In class she said she uses the word bioactivation for metabolism that creates something more noxious than what you were originally exposed to, and that some people say toxication. Metabolic activation is another name for it. Within A D M E T, the metabolism step can go two ways. Bioactivation is biotransformation of a potential poison to an active, harmful form. Detoxification is the opposite: metabolism to something less harmful and better able to be eliminated, which eliminates the toxicant or prevents it from forming. Acetaminophen can go either way, to N A P Q I or to a glucuronide or sulfate. Detoxification is wrong because it makes something less harmful. First-pass effect is wrong because it describes where metabolism happens after an oral dose, not what the metabolism produces. Conjugation to a glucuronide or a sulfate is wrong because that phase two metabolism of a normal acetaminophen dose is detoxification.",
"L13-018": "The enzyme that produces N A P Q I, the liver-toxic metabolite of acetaminophen, is cytochrome P four fifty two E one. Most of a normal daily dose is handled by two families of phase two enzymes, the glucuronosyl transferases and the sulfotransferases, which make a glucuronide or a sulfate. In class she called them G S T and S T for short. When those enzymes cannot keep up, the dose goes down the phase one P four fifty pathway, specifically two E one, which makes N A P Q I. She said the class already knew that N A P Q I is toxic to the liver. That spill-over is bioactivation. A glucuronosyl transferase is wrong because glucuronidation is the phase two route that detoxifies most of the dose. A sulfotransferase is wrong for the same reason. Glutathione S-transferase is wrong because, together with glutathione, it detoxifies N A P Q I after it forms; it does not make it. Her spoken G S T meant the glucuronosyl transferases, not this enzyme.",
"L13-019": "The total daily dose of acetaminophen that the Food and Drug Administration labeling says not to exceed is four grams in twenty four hours. A normal dose is detoxified by two families of phase two enzymes, the glucuronosyl transferases and the sulfotransferases. When those enzymes are overwhelmed, more of the dose goes through cytochrome P four fifty two E one, which makes N A P Q I, the metabolite that is toxic to the liver. In class she said that four grams is on the labeling, and that she tells her older parents not to take four grams in a twenty four hour period. Their phase two enzymes may be depleting in numbers, so the whole dose may not be handled by them, and some can spill over to N A P Q I. Two grams is wrong because it is below the labeled maximum, even though her advice for older adults is to stay under four grams. Six grams and eight grams are above the labeled maximum, where the phase two enzymes are less able to handle all of it and more goes to N A P Q I.",
"L13-020": "Two choices are correct: a genetic variant that lowers phase two enzyme activity, and induction of cytochrome P four fifty two E one by alcohol. Normally the glucuronosyl transferases and sulfotransferases handle a dose up to four grams. If a genetic variation reduces their activity, part of the dose cannot be handled by them and is pushed through two E one to N A P Q I. She pointed out that this is pharmacogenomics. Induction means the body makes more of an enzyme, and alcohol can induce two E one, so a normal dose makes more N A P Q I. Her example was her husband, who drank beers through a long weekend of baseball tournaments and then took acetaminophen for soreness, getting close to four grams. Inhibition of two E one by alcohol is wrong, because less enzyme activity would mean less N A P Q I; alcohol induces it. Increased glucuronosyl transferase activity is wrong, because more phase two activity detoxifies more of the dose. Either cause, or both together, can set up liver toxicity at the allowed dose.",
"L13-021": "The saying that the dose makes the poison is attributed to Paracelsus. In class she said he studied the toxic effects of compounds long ago. Dose is its own principle of toxicology because whether something poisons depends on how much is received, not only on what it is. Her table of lethal doses in rats runs from ethyl alcohol, at ten thousand milligrams per kilogram, down to botulinum toxin, at one hundred-thousandth of a milligram per kilogram. Alcohol is safe to drink at low doses, yet it can still poison at a high one. Casarett and Doull is the tempting answer, but they wrote the toxicology textbook her table and figures come from. Rodriguez-Mozaz is the first author of the article on pharmaceuticals in water. Hippocrates is not the name she gave.",
"L13-022": "The L D fifty is the dose that kills fifty percent of the animals given the compound. In class she called it the lethal dose that will kill off fifty percent of the animals given this compound, and her table gave L D fifty values in rats. It comes from a population dose-response study: animals receive different doses, deaths are grouped by dose, and the L D fifty is read where cumulative mortality reaches fifty percent, about one hundred milligrams per kilogram in the figure she read. The other choices are other values. The dose that gives the desired effect in fifty percent of the animals is the E D fifty, the effective dose. The dose that gives a toxic effect in fifty percent is the T D fifty, the toxic dose. The lowest dose that causes an effect is the threshold dose, the other value read from a population curve, about ten milligrams per kilogram in that figure.",
"L13-023": "Botulinum toxin has the lowest L D fifty in rats, one hundred-thousandth of a milligram per kilogram. The L D fifty is the dose that kills half the animals, so the smaller the number, the less compound it takes to kill and the more potent the poison. Her rat table ran from ethyl alcohol at ten thousand milligrams per kilogram down to botulinum toxin, a range she called massive. In class she said the dose makes the poison, but you have to know what compound you are talking about too, and that sometimes it only takes a tiny bit of something to be very harmful. Ethyl alcohol is wrong because the biggest number means the largest dose is needed, so it is the least potent poison listed. Nicotine, at one milligram per kilogram, is potent, but botulinum toxin is one hundred thousand times more so. Dioxin, at one thousandth of a milligram per kilogram, sits second from the bottom, and botulinum toxin is one hundred times more potent.",
"L13-024": "The can should be thrown away because it may hold botulinum toxin, and cooking does not destroy the toxin. The botulinum bacteria can get canned in with low-acid vegetables such as beans, peas and corn. They do not need oxygen, so they grow in the sealed can, and as they multiply they emit toxin and build up pressure, which is why the can squirts when it is opened. In class she said it is not the bacteria that kills you, but the botulinum toxin the bacteria make as they grow, and that you cannot heat or cook it to break the toxin down. So the claim that live bacteria cause the harm is wrong. The claim that cooking would concentrate the toxin is wrong, because the point is only that cooking fails to destroy it. The claim that the corn is too acidic is wrong, because acidic foods such as tomatoes are usually fine, and the risk is in low-acid vegetables. Botulinum toxin also has the lowest L D fifty on her rat table.",
"L13-025": "A plot of percent enzyme inhibition against dose is an individual dose-response relationship. It describes the response of individuals to varying doses. In her example, rodents were put into dosing groups, from no organophosphate to two and a half milligrams per kilogram, then five, and so on, and in each group the inhibition of carboxylesterase and of cholinesterase was measured. The y axis is percent inhibition and the x axis is dose, sometimes on a logarithmic scale to get linear lines. In class she said that many chemicals have more than one effect because they have multiple targets, that these organophosphate exposures inhibit two different enzymes, and that this could not be seen without graphing it as an individual dose-response curve. A population dose-response relationship is wrong because it groups responses such as deaths by dose and yields the L D fifty and threshold. A cumulative mortality curve is that population plot, and no deaths are counted here. A hormetic curve is U-shaped, with a protective low-dose effect, and nothing beneficial is measured here.",
"L13-026": "On the cumulative mortality curve, the L D fifty is read at fifty percent mortality, and the threshold is read at the first dose with an effect. The figure is a population dose-response: deaths are grouped by the dose the animals received, plotted as mortality frequency and as cumulative mortality. In the figure she read, fifty percent cumulative mortality fell at about one hundred milligrams per kilogram, so that is the L D fifty. The threshold is the dose where you first start seeing an effect, at the lowest dose with data, about ten milligrams per kilogram. The choice that swaps the two readings is wrong. Reading the threshold at the peak of the mortality frequency plot is wrong, because the threshold sits at the low-dose end, where effects first appear. Reading the L D fifty at one hundred percent mortality is wrong, because it is defined at fifty percent, and the threshold is the first dose with an effect, not a dose with none. She set aside the third panel, in probit units, saying nobody ever uses it.",
"L13-027": "The region of homeostasis is on the iron curve but not on the red wine curve. Iron is an essential nutrient. Too little causes deficiency and even death. The middle doses are the region of homeostasis, where the body has what it needs. Too much is toxic. Red wine is not essential, so its curve shows hormesis instead. A low dose may be protective, and a higher dose is harmful, with no region of need in between. In class she said that because red wine is not an essential nutrient, there is really no area of homeostasis, and the curve illustrates hormesis. Both curves are U-shaped, so the U shape does not separate them. Both have a toxic region at high doses. A protective effect at low doses belongs to the red wine curve, because at the lowest doses of iron the response is deficiency.",
"L13-028": "Hormesis describes a substance that is not essential, protective at a low dose and harmful at a higher dose. Her example is red wine on a Friday night: a glass or a glass and a half is possibly heart protective, and then she goes too far, gets a little tipsy, and should not be driving. The curve is U-shaped, but because red wine is not an essential nutrient, there is no region of homeostasis. She said hormesis is something toxicologists think about and probably nobody else does. The essential-substance choice, with deficiency below and toxicity above a range, is wrong because that is the essential-nutrient U-curve, such as iron, with homeostasis in the middle. A response from the first molecule with no threshold is wrong because that linear model is the theory for carcinogens. Building up to a toxic level with frequent doses is wrong because that depends on half-life and dosing frequency, as for compounds A and B, not on the shape of the response.",
"L13-029": "The linear, no-threshold dose-response is the model used for carcinogens. On this line, the first molecule of exposure could cause problems. In class she said that is a theory for exposure to carcinogens: maybe it takes just one molecule of a carcinogen, damaging your D N A and kicking you off into cancer. She qualified it with possibly, and said this is what was thought about carcinogens for the longest time. An S-shaped dose-response implies a threshold, a dose below which there is no effect, therapeutic or poisonous. That is probably the case for medications and for most poisons, so medications are wrong. Essential nutrients are wrong because they give a U-shaped curve with a region of homeostasis. Hormetic substances are wrong because hormesis is also U-shaped, not linear. Her caution was not to assume that everything has a dose that is not harmful.",
"L13-030": "The threshold fell because the thirty three month hamsters received a higher cumulative dose. In this classic study, hamsters were given two acetylaminofluorene, called two A A F, every day in dosing groups, and were sacrificed at twenty four or at thirty three months. The bladder-tumor threshold was about seventy five parts per million at twenty four months and about forty five parts per million at thirty three months. Cumulative was the keyword a student gave, and she confirmed it: animals that lived longer on the same daily dose received a higher total dose, so it did not take as much of the dose to reach threshold. A higher daily dose is wrong, because the dosing groups were the same and only the length of dosing differed. Faster metabolism with age is wrong, because the total dose received was the explanation. Old hamsters getting bladder tumors anyway is wrong, because that background finding was for liver tumors, not bladder tumors.",
"L13-031": "The tumors show that hamsters are a poor model for liver tumors, because old hamsters get liver tumors anyway. In the two A A F study, the group that received no compound and was sacrificed at thirty three months had a significant number of liver tumors. In class she asked whether cancer is a disease of the aged and answered yes, usually, so these old hamsters develop liver tumors as they age. She concluded that hamsters are not the best animals for testing whether two A A F causes liver cancer, because they get those tumors anyway. The finding is information about the animal's background physiology, not an anomaly. A dosing mistake in the controls is wrong, because it was a well-done study and the controls truly received no two A A F. The choice saying liver tumors have no threshold for two A A F is wrong, because tumors in a zero-dose group cannot come from a compound they never received. Spread from bladder tumors is wrong, because nothing pointed to spread and these animals had no compound to cause either tumor.",
"L13-032": "The margin of safety gives information about slope, and it is calculated as the T D one over the E D ninety nine. That is the dose toxic to one percent of the animals divided by the dose effective in ninety nine percent. In class she said that because the margin of safety uses a different percentage, it gives an idea of the slope of the dose-response curve, whereas the therapeutic index does not. The therapeutic index, usually the T D fifty over the E D fifty, is wrong here. Its percentage does not have to be fifty, but the numerator and the denominator must use the same percentage, so it reflects one point on each curve. The margin of exposure, the no observed adverse effect level, or N O A E L, over the exposure, is wrong because it is for repeated exposures to non-therapeutic compounds, not for slope. The E D ninety nine over the T D one is wrong because it puts the effective dose on top; the margin of safety divides the toxic dose by the effective dose.",
"L13-033": "The margin of exposure is used for repeated exposures to non-therapeutic compounds. In class she said it is used truly in toxicology, for exposures to something that is not therapeutic, such as a pollutant or an occupational exposure, and also for repeated exposures. It divides the no observed adverse effect level, which she pronounced Noel, by the chronic exposure. The N O A E L comes from animal exposure studies and is the highest dose at which the exposed animals showed no adverse response. The denominator is the exposure a human, or a critter in the wild, is estimated to have received. Comparing margins of exposure across compounds shows which contaminant is more dangerous, for example to decide which one to clean up first. Single doses of a therapeutic compound are wrong, because single doses use the therapeutic index or the margin of safety. Comparing slopes is wrong, because slope information comes from the margin of safety, the T D one over the E D ninety nine. Finding the dose that kills half the animals is the L D fifty, read from a population curve.",
"L13-034": "The correct statement is that the target organ may not be where the concentration is highest. Whether an organ is harmed depends on how sensitive it is as well as on how much compound reaches it. Her example: a compound may reach a higher concentration in muscle and cause no effect there, while the kidney, which may be super sensitive, is damaged at a lower concentration. In class she said this is probably a little counterintuitive. Even a sensitive organ has its own dose-response curve, so the concentration at the kidney still has to be high enough to cause an effect. That is why the dose at the target, not the dose given, determines toxicity. The statement that the target organ is where the concentration is highest is wrong for the same reason. The statement that the mechanism of action is the same at each target is wrong, because a compound that affects the kidney and the liver is likely to damage them by different mechanisms.",
"L13-035": "The main point of discharge of pharmaceuticals into surface waters, in the article, is wastewater treatment plant effluent. People take drugs and excrete the parent drug and its metabolites, and that waste goes to treatment plants. In class she said the current treatment does not get all the drugs out before the water goes back into streams, rivers and lakes, and that this water is a source for both humans and animals. Her four sources were human waste, improper disposal such as flushing unused antibiotics, livestock given hormones and antibiotics, and wastewater treatment plants. Pharmaceutical manufacturing plants are wrong; manufacturing is one source, but the article names treatment-plant effluent as the main discharge. Drinking-water treatment plant intakes are wrong, because that is where trace levels are found, not where the drugs enter. Landfills holding discarded medicines are wrong, because medicine thrown in the trash is only part of the supply, while drug excreted by patients and passed through treatment plants is the main discharge.",
"L13-036": "The article's two examples of harmed wildlife are vultures and fathead minnows. Vultures in India and Pakistan fed on carcasses of cattle treated with diclofenac and died of renal failure, because they could not excrete the drug. Fathead minnows in a lake deliberately dosed with low levels of ethinyl estradiol showed cellular and tissue responses and then a population collapse by the second year. These two examples come from the article; in class her wildlife example was frogs, fish and alligators turning more female where estrogen levels are higher. Humans drinking treated water are wrong, because humans are not wildlife and drinking-water levels are in parts per trillion, below a therapeutic dose. Cattle and poultry on feedlots are wrong, because livestock given drugs are a source; the cattle were the source of the diclofenac, not the harmed organisms. Bacteria in lake sediments are wrong, because antibiotic resistance there is a separate concern, not one of the two examples.",
"L13-037": "Her key is true: a compound cleared from the body before the next exposure may still result in chronic adverse effects. This is compound C on the frequency figure. Its short half-life clears each dose before the next, so it never accumulates into the toxic blood concentration. But clearing between doses only rules out build-up, not every adverse response. She listed ways C can still harm with repeated use. An allergy would not take very much drug. C might go to a different target site from the one the curve describes. It could synergize with other drugs and give additive adverse effects. And a drug injected subcutaneously or intradermally can give a therapeutic response while damaging the injection site, which she called an adverse response that has nothing to do with half-life. She said C is not necessarily all good to go. False is wrong because it assumes that staying below the toxic blood concentration makes the compound safe.",
"L13-038": "Chronic exposure is repeated exposure for more than three months, up to years. That is her key. The four durations come from animal studies. Acute is less than twenty four hours, subacute is less than one month, subchronic is one to three months, and chronic is more than three months. One to three months is wrong because that window is subchronic, not chronic. Less than a month is subacute, and less than twenty four hours is acute. In animal studies the duration is set by design, so the effects can be tied to it. People are exposed acutely and chronically too, but often do not realize it or cannot remember when it started. In class she said toxicologists still try to sort human exposures into these categories, to see what kind of response the person is having based on that duration of exposure.",
"L13-039": "The value with the formula toxic dose over therapeutic dose is the therapeutic index. That is her key. By convention it is the toxic dose in fifty percent of the animals over the therapeutic dose in fifty percent. In class she said the percentage does not have to be fifty, but the numerator and the denominator must use the same percentage. Because both are read at the same percentage, the therapeutic index carries no slope information. A wide therapeutic index means less chance of toxicity, and a narrow one means the toxic dose is not much higher than the therapeutic dose. The margin of safety is wrong because it is the T D one over the E D ninety nine, two different percentages, which is why it shows slope. The margin of exposure is wrong because it is the no observed adverse effect level over the exposure, used for repeated exposures to non-therapeutic compounds. The threshold dose is wrong because it is read from the curve as the first dose with an effect, not calculated as a ratio.",
"L13-040": "A D M E stands for absorption, distribution, metabolism, excretion. That is her key. Toxicology adds a T for toxicity, making A D M E T. In class she described the path: anything that gets into the body is absorbed in some way, or injected, then distributed throughout the body, likely metabolized, and then excreted. The step toxicologists watch most is metabolism, because it can turn a compound into something nastier, called bioactivation, as acetaminophen becomes N A P Q I, or into something less harmful and easier to eliminate, called detoxification. The expansion that starts with administration is wrong, because the A is absorption. The expansion with mechanism in place of metabolism is wrong, because the M is metabolism. The expansion with dose, mechanism and exposure is wrong, because those are other principles of toxicology, not letters of A D M E.",
"L13-041": "Her key is false: the mechanism of a drug's toxicity is not always the same as the mechanism of its efficacy. In class she said that sometimes the target of something toxic is not the same target as its therapeutic target. Acetaminophen is her example. It is taken for soreness, but its liver toxicity comes from N A P Q I, a metabolite made by cytochrome P four fifty two E one when the phase two enzymes cannot handle the dose. A compound can also have multiple targets, with a different mechanism at each. She explained that when the effective, toxic and lethal dose-response lines for one compound are not parallel, the mechanisms leading to effectiveness, toxicity and death are likely different. This is why many drugs have side effects unrelated to their therapeutic action; her example was the ototoxicity of the aminoglycosides. True is wrong because it assumes one drug acts at one target by one mechanism.",
"L13-042": "Her key is false: the concentration of a medication is not always highest at its target site. The target organ may not be the site of highest concentration. Her example: a compound may be at a higher concentration in muscle without harming it, while the kidney, which may be super sensitive, is damaged at a lower concentration. In class she called this probably a little counterintuitive, because people assume harm happens wherever the concentration is highest. The dose at the target still decides toxicity. Even the sensitive kidney has its own dose-response curve, so if the concentration there is not high enough, it may not cause a problem. True is wrong because concentration alone does not decide where the harm occurs; the sensitivity of the tissue matters too. This is the target and mechanism of action principle.",
"L13-043": "The greatest source is consumption of medications by humans and the resulting waste streams. That is her key. Every person who takes a medication excretes the parent drug and its metabolites, so ordinary use feeds the waste stream from a whole community. That sewage reaches wastewater treatment plants, which, she said, do not get all the drugs out before the water goes back into streams, rivers and lakes. Flushing unused medications down the toilet is wrong; it is improper disposal and a real source, but it involves only unused medicine. Medications thrown in the trash and leaching from landfills are wrong for the same reason, because only leftover medicine is discarded. Pharmaceutical manufacturing is wrong because it contributes, but the drug consumed and excreted by people everywhere outweighs it. Her other sources were livestock given hormones and antibiotics, and the treatment plants themselves.",
"L13-044": "Her key is false: the dose of pharmaceuticals people get from drinking water does not exceed the therapeutic daily dose. Drinking water is the main way people are exposed, and the drugs measured in it are usually at parts per trillion. In class she asked whether someone who drinks plenty of water a day from such a source gets up to a normal therapeutic daily dose, and the answer was no. So she said the worried patient is probably OK. The concern shifts to those exposed continuously or more sensitive. Wildlife live in the water and are exposed by drinking, eating, swimming and through the skin, and their absorption, distribution, metabolism and excretion, dose and targets are unknown. Sensitive people include those who are immunocompromised or have disease states, the elderly, children, and the fetus in utero. True is wrong because finding drugs in the water does not mean the levels approach a prescription dose.",
"L13-045": "The authors concluded that more data is needed to determine whether pharmaceuticals in water are adversely affecting human health. That is her key. She described the meeting's message as needing more research to figure out how bad the problem is and who is being affected. People are exposed mainly through drinking water, at parts per trillion, below a daily therapeutic dose, and the metabolites of many drugs have not been studied, so harm to human health can be neither shown nor ruled out. For wildlife, adverse effects are documented; in class her example was frogs, fish and alligators shifting to more females where estrogen levels are higher. So the claim that pharmaceuticals in water are not affecting aquatic wildlife is wrong. The claim that they are adversely affecting human health is wrong, because the authors did not conclude that. No answers are correct is wrong, because the authors did reach a conclusion: the data are not yet enough to decide.",
"L13-046": "The five principles of toxicology are source, exposure, A D M E T, dose, and target and mechanism of action. That is her key. Source is where the poison comes from, exposure is how it reaches the body, A D M E T is what the body does with it, dose is how much, and target and mechanism is where and how it harms. The tempting list swaps in route, metabolism and receptor; but route is part of exposure, metabolism is part of A D M E T, and receptor is not the principle's name. Toxin, toxicant, xenobiotic and poison are the definitions, not the principles, and half-life, L D fifty and threshold are values inside the exposure and dose principles.",
"L14-001": "Compound A is the more potent. Potency is read along the dose axis. For the same measured response, the compound that needs less dose to produce it is the more potent one. In the left panel, A starts producing the response at a lower dose than B, so A is more potent. Because the comparison is made at the same response, no toxic or lethal value is needed. In class she said the class could already tell her that A is most potent, because you start getting the effect being measured at a lower dose compared to B. B needs a higher dose before the response appears, so it is less potent. The two are not equally potent, because the curves start at different doses. A switch in potency, with A ahead at low doses and B at high doses, belongs to the C and D panel, where C levels off and the lines cross. The A and B lines do not cross.",
"L14-002": "C is more potent at low doses, and D is more potent once the curves cross. This panel is her trick question. C starts responding at a lower dose than D, so at low doses C is more potent. As the dose rises, C's response levels out, while D keeps a linear response across the doses tested. Once C has leveled off and D's line crosses it, D is the more potent compound. In class she said you cannot say C is always more potent than D. You can in the lower doses, but once C has leveled off and D's line is crossing it, D is more potent than C. So potency can change places with dose. Calling C more potent at every dose ignores the leveling off above the crossing point. Calling D more potent at every dose ignores that C responds first at low doses. Putting D ahead at low doses and C ahead after the cross has the order backwards.",
"L14-003": "Saturated receptors, saturated bioactivating enzymes, a saturated gut transporter, and a change in stomach pH could all explain why C's curve bends over. Any step between dose and response that can be saturated will flatten the curve. When all the receptors C binds are fully occupied, more dose gives no more response. If C is bioactivated to something that is still active, that metabolism can saturate, and no more turnover occurs. If C is an oral drug actively absorbed across the gut by P-glycoprotein, the transporter can be saturated. A rising dose of C could also change the pH in the stomach and alter its own absorption. She also accepted that C may hit the same receptor as D in a different way, so that C saturates and D does not. In class she said target, binding, metabolism, distribution and absorption could all change C's potency with dose. A dose range that was too wide does not belong. A wide range is what reveals the bend. It does not cause it.",
"L14-004": "A study that stops too early risks missing that C and D change places in potency. At low doses C gives the response first, so C is more potent there. At higher doses C's curve levels off while D keeps rising, and after the lines cross, D is more potent. If the doses are cut off before C bends over, nobody sees where C's curve is going. In class she said that if we did not extend the doses far enough, we might miss the fact that their potency changes places. So a dose-response study needs a wide range of doses. The threshold of C sits at the low end of the curve, which the shortened study still covers. C being more potent at low doses is also in the part that was covered. The lethal dose in half the animals is a different measurement and was not the point.",
"L14-005": "From the lowest doses to the highest, the three lines are the effective dose, the toxic dose, and the lethal dose. As dose rises, one compound can produce more than one kind of response. The first to appear is the intended one, the effective or therapeutic dose, labeled E D. At higher doses a toxic response appears, labeled T D. At higher doses still comes death, the lethal dose, labeled L D. Each outcome has its own dose-response line. In class she said effective or therapeutic dose is first, then a toxic response, and then a lethal dose. Because the three lines are not parallel, the mechanisms behind effect, toxicity and death can differ. Putting the toxic dose first is wrong, because the therapeutic response appears before the toxic one. Putting the lethal dose first is wrong, because death is the last outcome to appear. Putting lethal before toxic is wrong, because toxicity comes before lethality as dose rises.",
"L14-006": "Non-parallel lines suggest that different mechanisms produce the compound's effect, its toxicity and its death. Most people expect rising dose to carry a compound straight from effective to toxic to lethal, with parallel lines. When a student offered differences in A D M E, meaning absorption, distribution, metabolism and excretion, she accepted it, then said the more important explanation is the last principle of toxicology, target and mechanism of action. In class she said that when the curves are not parallel, it leads you to believe the mechanisms leading to those three outcomes are different, so the compound causes toxicity in a different way than it causes effectiveness, and may cause death in yet another way. One mechanism at one target would be expected to give parallel lines. The three lines come from one compound with three measured outcomes, not from three compounds. Dose range was the point of the C and D panel, not this figure.",
"L14-007": "The therapeutic index is conventionally the T D fifty over the E D fifty. T D fifty is the dose toxic to fifty percent of the animals, and E D fifty is the dose effective in fifty percent. The toxic dose goes on top, so a wide index means the toxic dose is well above the therapeutic dose and there is less chance of toxicity. A narrow index means the toxic dose is not much higher. Another percentage can be used, as long as the numerator and the denominator use the same one. In class she said that the toxic dose in fifty percent of the animals over the therapeutic dose in fifty percent is the usual convention. E D fifty over T D fifty is the same ratio upside down. T D one over E D ninety-nine is the margin of safety. The no observed adverse effect level over the exposure is the margin of exposure.",
"L14-008": "When a percentage other than fifty is used, both doses must be read at the same percentage. The therapeutic index compares the toxic and therapeutic curves at one matching point, conventionally the dose that affects fifty percent of the animals on each. In class she said it does not always have to be fifty percent. It could be a different percentage, but the numerator and the denominator need to have the same percentage. Using different percentages makes a different value. The toxic dose for one percent of the animals over the effective dose for ninety-nine percent is the margin of safety, and that difference in percentage is what brings in the slope of the curve. So a toxic dose at one percent with an effective dose at ninety-nine is not the index, and neither is any pairing where the toxic dose uses a higher percentage than the effective dose. The margin of exposure is not a renamed index. It is the no observed adverse effect level over an exposure, with no percentages of animals.",
"L14-009": "The margin of safety gives an idea of the slope, because it uses two different percentages, the T D one and the E D ninety-nine. The margin of safety is the toxic dose for one percent of the animals over the effective dose for ninety-nine percent. Like the therapeutic index, it can be used for a therapeutic compound. In class she said that because a different percentage is used, it gives you an idea of the slope of your dose-response curve, whereas the therapeutic index does not. The therapeutic index uses the T D fifty and the E D fifty, one point on each graph, so it carries no slope information. The margin of exposure is the no observed adverse effect level over an exposure, a comparison of a no-effect dose with an exposure, not a slope measure. The L D fifty, the dose lethal to fifty percent of the animals, is a single point and also carries no slope information.",
"L14-010": "The margin of exposure is used for repeated exposures to a non-therapeutic compound. In class she said it is used truly in toxicology, for exposures to something that is not therapeutic, such as a pollutant or an occupational exposure, and for repeated exposures. A pollutant has no therapeutic dose to compare against. So the margin of exposure puts the N O A E L, the no observed adverse effect level from animal exposure studies, over the chronic exposure that people or wildlife are calculated to receive. Margins for different contaminants are then compared to judge which is more dangerous. A single dose of a medication uses the therapeutic index or the margin of safety instead. Slope information comes from the margin of safety, the T D one over the E D ninety-nine. Comparing the potency of two medications means comparing the doses that give one response, which is a different question.",
"L14-011": "The N O A E L, the no observed adverse effect level, comes from animal studies. It is the highest dose tested in the animals that did not cause an adverse response. She pronounces it Noel. In class she said it is a dose used in animal exposure studies of the compound at which the exposed animals did not have an adverse response. At one point she phrased it as the lowest such dose, but the toxicology textbook by Casarett and Doull defines it as the highest dose that does not cause significant toxicity, and highest is the correct reading. In the margin of exposure it is the numerator. The denominator is the exposure that people or wildlife are estimated to receive. Human surveys are not the source. The dose that kills half the animals is the L D fifty. A concentration measured in water belongs to the exposure, in the denominator.",
"L14-012": "The denominator is the total exposure the people at the site have received. The margin of exposure is the N O A E L, the no observed adverse effect level, over the chronic exposure. The N O A E L is generated in animal exposure studies and goes in the numerator. The denominator is calculated by the assessor as how much total exposure a human, or a critter in the wild, has gotten to the compound. In class she said the N O A E L is what we generate in animal exposure studies, and for the denominator we try to figure out how much total exposure a human has gotten to that compound. Putting the N O A E L in the denominator reverses the ratio. The effective dose in ninety-nine percent of the animals is the denominator of the margin of safety, not this value. A pollutant has no therapeutic dose in humans, which is why the margin of exposure is used for it.",
"L14-013": "The assessor calculates a margin of exposure for each contaminant, compares the values, and cleans up the more dangerous compound. Each margin sets the compound's N O A E L, the no observed adverse effect level from animal studies, against the exposure people at the site actually receive, so it reflects both how toxic the compound is and how much of it people get. One value means little alone. Its real use is comparing one compound with another to estimate how bad each is. In class she said you would calculate the margin of exposure, figure out which compound is more dangerous to the humans exposed, and might decide to spend your money cleaning up that more dangerous compound. A high N O A E L by itself only means the animals tolerated a high dose; the ratio with exposure is what matters. Soil concentration alone ignores how much exposure it takes to cause harm. A therapeutic index cannot be calculated for a pollutant, which has no therapeutic dose.",
"L14-014": "A drug with a narrow therapeutic index has a toxic dose close to its therapeutic dose. The therapeutic index is the toxic dose over the therapeutic dose. In class she said there is less chance of toxicity for the drug with a wide therapeutic index, and that a narrow therapeutic index means the toxic dose is not too much higher than the therapeutic dose. So the narrow-index drug carries more chance of toxicity, and the index tells how much error in dosing can be tolerated. The same comparison logic applies to margins of safety and margins of exposure. Less chance of toxicity belongs to the wide index, not the narrow one. Tolerating more dosing error also belongs to the wide index. A steeper dose-response curve cannot be read from the therapeutic index, which gives no slope information. The margin of safety is the value that reflects slope.",
"L14-015": "The therapeutic index is the T D fifty over the E D fifty. The margin of safety is the T D one over the E D ninety-nine. The margin of exposure is the N O A E L, the no observed adverse effect level, over the chronic exposure. The therapeutic index puts the toxic dose over the therapeutic dose at the same percentage, usually fifty, one point on each graph, so it gives no slope information. The margin of safety uses different percentages on top and bottom, so it gives an idea of the slope. Both apply to therapeutic compounds. The margin of exposure applies to non-therapeutic compounds, such as pollutants, with repeated exposures. Its numerator comes from animal studies, and its denominator is the exposure people receive. In class she said the therapeutic index looks at efficacy compared to toxicity, usually using fifty percent of animals, and the margin of exposure is for non-therapeutic compounds with repeated exposures. All three are most useful for comparing compounds with each other. The pair most easily confused is the index and the margin of safety: one shared percentage is the index, two different percentages is the margin of safety.",
"L14-016": "The kidney is more sensitive, and harm need not occur where the concentration is highest. The compound is most concentrated in muscle, with no adverse effect there, yet it damages the kidney at a lower concentration. In class she said that where a compound has its adverse effect may not be where its highest concentration is, which is counterintuitive, and that maybe the kidney is just super sensitive compared to the muscle. Even so, the dose at the kidney still determines toxicity. A super-sensitive kidney is not harmed unless the concentration there gets high enough, because there is still a dose-response curve at the kidney. Assuming harm must occur at the highest concentration is the intuition she called wrong, so neither the kidney level nor the muscle level was misread. Calling the damage idiosyncratic does not fit either. Idiosyncratic means unexplained, and here the explanation is the kidney's greater sensitivity.",
"L14-017": "The compound damages the kidney only when its concentration in the kidney gets high enough. The dose at the place where the compound acts determines toxicity. In class she said that even if the kidney is super sensitive, the compound may not cause a problem there if it does not get high enough. Even the most sensitive places need a high enough concentration, and there is still a dose-response curve right at the kidney. Sensitivity lowers the dose needed, but it does not mean that any dose reaching the kidney, however low, will harm it. The kidney does not have to be the site of highest concentration, because where a compound harms need not be where it is most concentrated. Repeated versus single exposure was not the point. The dose at the target was.",
"L14-018": "The kidney and liver mechanisms are likely different. When one compound hits several targets, each target is affected in its own way. In class she said that if a compound affects the kidney and the liver, it is likely causing damage to those two organs in different ways, one mechanism in the kidney and one mechanism in the liver. The non-parallel effective, toxic and lethal curves make the same point, since different slopes point to different mechanisms. Finding two damaged organs also does not mean every target is known; some places a compound hits may not have been identified yet. The same mechanism in both organs is unlikely, because different targets usually mean different mechanisms. The organ with the higher concentration is not the true target. Both are targets, and concentration does not decide which organ is harmed. The toxic mechanism need not match the therapeutic one, because toxic targets and mechanisms can differ from therapeutic ones.",
"L14-019": "Over-diuresis is the therapy gone too far, while aminoglycoside ototoxicity comes from a target unrelated to the therapeutic one. Many side effects in a drug list have nothing to do with the drug's therapeutic action, and the target-and-mechanism principle is the reason. Some side effects are the therapeutic action overshooting. Her example was a diuretic that takes so much fluid off that the patient needs hydration again. Others come from a target of the adverse effect that is very different from the therapeutic target and mechanism. In class she said that why aminoglycosides cause ototoxicity is weird stuff, the classic example of how the target of an adverse effect can be very different from the therapeutic target. Sometimes the mechanism of an adverse effect is known or can be found, and sometimes it is not known. The reversed pairing is wrong, because dehydration is the diuretic's own action going too far. Ototoxicity is not therapy gone too far, because killing bacteria has nothing to do with hearing.",
"L14-020": "The meeting was about drugs contaminating water, and it was attended by government agencies such as the E P A, the Environmental Protection Agency, along with academics, the pharmaceutical industry, and other scientists interested in the health of the planet. It was an environmental health summit in two thousand eight, the first time these groups met about the finding that drugs are contaminating the environment, particularly water. Their purpose was to work out what is known, how bad the problem is, and what to do about it. The paper's answer on what to do was more research and educating scientists and the public. In class she said it was an environmental health summit attended by government organizations such as the E P A, academicians, the pharmacy industry and other scientists. Livestock came up only as one source of drugs in water. Drinking water treatment methods and antibiotic resistance were related concerns, not the subject of the summit.",
"L14-021": "A pharmacy student needs to know because patients who hear about drugs in the water supply will come to the pharmacist asking whether the water is dangerous. She gave two reasons for the paper. It is a good way to explore the principles of toxicology, and patients will read an article or hear a podcast about drugs in the water and come in worried, for example about drinking someone else's Prozac. In class she said patients might come to you freaking out, and the goal is to be able to help them calm down or be concerned. The facts behind that answer are that drugs in drinking water are at about parts per trillion, far below a therapeutic dose. Pharmacists do not test the community's tap water; scientists sampled the waters after the summit. A patient cannot get a therapeutic dose from tap water, even when well hydrated. Flushing unused drugs is only one source; her practice quiz key names consumption of medications by humans and the resulting waste streams as the greatest source.",
"L14-022": "The discussion covers surface water, groundwater and drinking water sources. Surface water means lakes, streams and rivers. Groundwater is water held underground, such as the Edwards Aquifer. Drinking water sources are the waters that drinking water is drawn from. These are the waters where drugs end up and where people and animals meet them. Treated wastewater that still carries drugs flows back into surface water, which is how the drugs reach the other waters. In class she said we are talking about surface water, groundwater, and drinking water sources. Hospital wastewater and factory effluent are discharges from single facilities, not the waters people and wildlife are exposed to. Sewage sludge, called biosolids, is the solid left after treatment, not a kind of water. Seawater, rainwater and bottled water are not among the three.",
"L14-023": "The sources are drug and metabolites excreted by people taking medications, unused medications flushed down the toilet, waste from medicated livestock on feedlots, and treatment plant discharge that still holds drugs. People take medications and excrete both the parent drug and its metabolites. Unused medications, such as leftover antibiotics, are dumped down the toilet. Livestock on feedlots and in chicken houses are given hormones and antibiotics, which come out in their waste. Treatment plants do not remove all the drugs before the water returns to streams, rivers and lakes. Her key notes that flushing could be reduced by other disposal methods, animal waste by controls on feedlots and farms, and human waste cannot be reduced, because people take medications. The sources are the same for wildlife as for humans. In class she said we take the drugs, we flush, we improperly dispose of our meds, we have animals on feedlots, and the treatment plants do not take care of all the drugs. Discharge from drug manufacturing plants does not belong. Every source she named traces drugs after their use.",
"L14-024": "The water that treatment plants release is a source of exposure for both humans and wildlife. Water from flushing is treated, but current treatment does not get all the drugs out before it goes back into streams, rivers and lakes. In class she said the water from flushing gets treated a bit, but the current mechanisms do not get all the drugs out, and she answered her own question, a source for humans or animals, with both. Wildlife but not humans is wrong, because drinking water sources draw on the same surface water and groundwater. Humans but not wildlife is wrong, because aquatic animals live in the receiving streams and lakes. Neither group is wrong, because the discharge still carries drugs. The sources of drugs in water are the same for humans and for animals in the wild.",
"L14-025": "Humans are exposed mostly by drinking the water, while aquatic wildlife are exposed by drinking, by breathing through gills, through the skin, and by eating. For people the main route is drinking water. Her key also lists eating fish and recreational skin contact, and says that human duration and frequency depend on how people are exposed and where they live. Animals that live in or near the water are exposed constantly. Fish breathe the water across their gills, frogs absorb a lot through their skin, and animals eat other animals that live in the water. Her key says wildlife are exposed for their entire life, repeatedly, and constantly if their home range stays contaminated, and it judges aquatic wildlife exposure the more significant. In class she said they get exposed drinking, eating, swimming, living in it. Wildlife are not exposed mostly by drinking alone. Bathing, swimming and eating fish are possible human routes, but none is the main one, and gills are only one of several wildlife routes.",
"L14-026": "Most medications in drinking water were found at about parts per trillion. In class she said the concentrations are about parts per trillion, and rarely higher. Parts per billion would be a thousand times higher, and parts per million a million times higher, than what was found. That number drives her answer on dose. Even a person who drinks a lot of water does not get anywhere near a normal therapeutic daily dose, so the levels are not therapeutic levels. Local levels can run higher. Estrogen is higher along the shorelines of big cities where many young women may be taking oral contraceptives, and she named Miami, Houston, San Diego and San Francisco.",
"L14-027": "A D M E, dose and target are known for humans, except for many metabolites, and little is known for wildlife. For humans, drug development studies established how these drugs work, their absorption, distribution, metabolism and excretion, and their dose. But many drugs are metabolized to substances nobody has identified, so those metabolites have no data. For wildlife we rarely know any of it. The animals used in drug development are rodents, guinea pigs, dogs and sometimes pigs, not sea otters, orca whales, fish or mollusks. In class she said those species are not used in drug development studies, so we do not know how drugs made and tested for humans will act in them. Her key adds that there are some human data from drug development, and very little if any data on harmful metabolites in aquatic wildlife, because those studies have not been done. Knowing the human metabolites is wrong, because many are unknown. Field data on wildlife do not exist. The test species are not fish, frogs or whales.",
"L14-028": "The best answer is that the patient is probably OK, because parts per trillion is far below a therapeutic dose. In class she asked whether the parts per trillion in drinking water would get a well-hydrated person up to a normal therapeutic daily dose, answered no, and concluded that for humans we can be OK. That does not mean ignoring sensitive populations: the fetus in utero, children, the elderly and people with diseases. Her former work in risk assessment has not found these levels to be a problem for humans, and if concentrations start rising, risk assessors would re-evaluate. Her key lists what is still unknown for humans: the effects of low-dose chronic exposures, good data on sensitive populations, and how multiple exposures act. Reaching a daily therapeutic dose is wrong at any realistic water intake. Saying nothing is known is wrong, because data exist from drug development and the dose is probably below therapeutic. Reassurance based on treatment plants is wrong, because treatment does not remove all the drugs.",
"L14-029": "The sensitive populations are the fetus in utero, the elderly, children, and people with diseases or immune compromise. Even though humans are probably OK at parts per trillion, these groups should not be ignored. When she asked who should worry, the immunocompromised, people with disease states and the young in utero were offered, and she accepted them. In class she said we should not ignore our sensitive populations, which are in utero, the elderly, kids, and people with diseases. Risk assessment on in utero exposure is very hard to do, and no articles have shown a child affected in utero by drugs in the water supply. Her key says good data on sensitive populations are lacking, naming the fetus, children, the elderly and people with underlying disease states. Healthy adults who drink a lot of water do not belong. The well-hydrated adult was her example showing that the dose stays far below therapeutic.",
"L14-030": "The evidence is frogs, some fish and alligators shifting toward female where estrogen levels are higher. Populations change from being about half male and half female to more female in those places, which she called a red flag. To the question of whether wild animals are having problems, she said both answers, yes and we do not know, are right. For some species there is evidence. For other wildlife, such as orca whales, we do not know, because they are hard to catch and study. In class she said you start seeing populations of frogs, some fish and alligators change from half male, half female to more female where estrogen levels are higher. Fish growing larger below treatment plants was not described; the effect was on sex ratio. Orca whales are the example of what we do not know. The shift is toward female and tied to estrogen, not toward male near feedlots.",
"L14-031": "The evidence is isolated exposure studies with estrogenic compounds that show the same shift. To the question of whether warming water could be causing the shift toward female, her answer was that some of these species have been exposed in isolated studies to certain doses of hormones or estrogen-like compounds, and the shift is seen. Estrogen levels are also known to be high in the places where the shift occurs, so an association is reasonable. In class she said other studies are still needed to see if warming water is causing that shift, and that it could be both. So warming has not been ruled out by temperature studies. Drug development studies in rodents and dogs tell little about wildlife and are not the evidence. Antibiotics in sediments are tied to resistant bacteria, not to the shift toward female.",
"L14-032": "The concern she called a real problem is bacteria in waters and sediments becoming resistant to antibiotics. Antibiotics that are improperly disposed of reach lakes and streams. The many microbes that live in the sediments and in beach sand are exposed, survive, and become resistant. A person who gets a cut at the beach could then have an infection that is hard to treat. In class she said this is a really big concern that most people know about, and she repeated that it is a problem. The other three are not real problems today. Drinking water is at parts per trillion, far below a therapeutic concentration. Feminization was described in frogs, fish and alligators, not in people. The fetus is a sensitive population to watch, but no articles show a child harmed in the womb by drugs in the water supply.",
"L14-033": "Nerve damage that appears days after an organophosphate exposure is a delayed effect. The person first has the expected immediate effects, the cholinergic ones. Then some organophosphates, depending on their structure, affect different enzymes in the nerves and cause a neuropathy days later. One such compound is tri-ortho-cresyl phosphate. In class she called it a delayed effect by about days, not years. The immediate effect is the cholinergic toxicity, not the later neuropathy. It is not a local effect, because the nerve damage occurs away from the site of first contact. It is not a reversible effect, because nerve cells cannot divide and be replaced, so nerve damage is considered irreversible.",
"L14-034": "Diethylstilbestrol, called D E S, is delayed by years, and the organophosphate neuropathy by days. D E S was given in the nineteen fifties to help women maintain a pregnancy. The harm appeared in their daughters, exposed in the womb, as a very particular vaginal cancer when those girls became young adults. Some organophosphate pesticides give the expected immediate effects and then, depending on their structure, nerve damage that appears days after the exposure, because they affect different enzymes in the nerves. In class she described the D E S cancer as a very delayed response, and said of the organophosphate neuropathy that it is a delayed effect by about days, not years. Days and years is the same pair reversed. Years and years is wrong, because the nerve damage appears in days. Days and days is wrong, because the D E S cancers took years.",
"L14-035": "What determines whether a toxic injury is reversible is the tissue's ability to regenerate. The liver is unique: it can heal and regenerate itself, so many liver injuries are reversible, though mild injury often regenerates and severe chronic injury may not. Most nerve and C N S cells, the cells of the central nervous system, cannot divide or be regenerated, so nerve damage should be considered irreversible. Cancer and birth defects are also considered irreversible. One exposure can produce both kinds of injury. In class she said the liver is really unique, able to regenerate when it becomes damaged, while nerve cells cannot divide and their injury is permanent. Blood flow is not the deciding property; the contrast is whether damaged cells can be replaced. The concentration of toxicant decides whether injury occurs, not whether the tissue recovers. Immediate versus delayed is a separate general concept.",
"L14-036": "Cancer is still irreversible because the cancerous cells were killed, not returned to normal. A cancer can be cut out, or killed with x-rays or chemotherapy. But the cells that became cancerous never switched back to being normal cells. Treatment removes them. In class she said that the cells that became cancerous did not switch back to being normal; we killed them, we got rid of them. Birth defects are irreversible for a related reason. A limb that formed wrongly during fetal development cannot be made to form again. The reversed statement, that the cells were returned to normal, is the opposite of what treatment does. Delay and reversibility are separate ideas, so being a delayed effect does not make cancer irreversible. Regeneration explains why many liver injuries are reversible, but cancer is irreversible in any organ.",
"L14-037": "A highly reactive agent like chlorine gas reacts where it first touches the body and often is never absorbed. A local effect occurs at the site of first contact, such as acid splashed on the skin or chlorine gas breathed into the lungs, eyes and nasal mucosa. A systemic effect requires absorption at the site of entry and distribution into the circulation. In class she said most toxins cause systemic effects unless they are highly reactive, and that very reactive compounds like chlorine gas and acids cause the local effect and often will not have a chance to go systemic. Being absorbed too quickly to act locally is the opposite of what happens. It is not true that most toxins act locally; most cause systemic effects, and the highly reactive ones are the exception. Needing distribution to a site of action is what a systemic effect requires, not a local one.",
"L14-038": "The tetraethyl lead case illustrates a poison with both a local and a systemic effect. A toxicologist got a couple of drops on her glove. The compound got through the glove and burned her skin, which was the local effect. It was then absorbed through the burned skin and affected her central nervous system, and days later she died of neurological problems, which was the systemic effect. In class she said a burn mark was found on her hand where the compound got through the glove, that it was absorbed through that burned skin and affected her central nervous system, and that it is definitely a neurotoxin. The right protective equipment matters with such a compound. A local effect alone is wrong, because death from neurotoxicity is systemic. A systemic effect with no local injury is wrong, because of the burn. An indirect systemic effect through fluid loss is the acid burn example; here the compound itself was absorbed.",
"L14-039": "A local acid burn produces kidney problems because fluid leaks from the burned skin, blood volume is disturbed, and the kidneys suffer. This is an indirect systemic effect. An acid burn over a large body surface area behaves like a thermal burn: the skin can no longer hold the body's fluids in. In class she said you run the risk of having your blood volume really messed up because you are leaking fluids, and people can end up with really bad kidney problems as the body fights to keep the fluids in, just as with a thermal burn. That is apart from the risk of infection. So a burned patient must be looked at systemically, not only for skin healing. The acid is not absorbed to attack the kidney directly; the harm comes through fluid loss. No second exposure is needed. Highly reactive agents act where they touch and often are not absorbed and distributed.",
"L14-040": "The kinds of interaction are additive, synergistic, potentiation and antagonistic. People are rarely exposed to one thing at a time. Contaminated foods, additives and preservatives, drugs, hobbies and the workplace all add exposures, and each probably has its own mechanism and target. The compounds can change each other's absorption, protein binding, biotransformation or excretion, so the combined response may be increased or decreased. In class she said you could have additive or synergistic or potentiation or antagonistic interactions going on, which makes it really hard to predict how bad it will be for people or animals. Dispositional does not belong. It is a type of tolerance, the kind based on A D M E, not an interaction between compounds.",
"L14-041": "It is hard to put a number on the risk because the interactions among the compounds are hard to quantify. Risk assessment of a polluted site means sampling it, learning which compounds are there and at what concentrations in the ground, water or air, working out how people use the site, whether walking across it, living in it or working in it, and then calculating how risky the whole thing is. The exposures probably have different mechanisms and targets, and they can interact additively, synergistically, by potentiation, or antagonistically. In class she said, from her own risk assessment work, that it is very hard to get a number that quantifies the risk with multiple exposures, because it is really hard to quantify these interactions. Measuring concentrations is not the problem, since sampling measures them. Animal studies supply a no observed adverse effect level for each compound. One shared mechanism is wrong; the different mechanisms are part of the difficulty.",
"L14-042": "Dispositional tolerance is based on absorption, distribution, metabolism and excretion, while reduced responsiveness is pharmacodynamic. Tolerance is a decreased response to a toxic effect after earlier exposure. In dispositional tolerance, less of the toxicant reaches the site of the toxic effect. Her examples are carbon tetrachloride, which inhibits its own metabolism to a toxic product, and cadmium, which induces a metal-carrying protein that helps the kidneys eliminate it. In reduced responsiveness, the target itself responds less, for example when receptors are down-regulated. In class she said the dispositional kind is based on A D M E, and the reduced responsiveness kind is pharmacodynamic, dealing with receptors. The reversed pairing is the tempting answer. Both kinds occur with medications and with pollutants, and both develop with repeated exposure.",
"L14-043": "Tolerance to carbon tetrachloride develops because it inhibits its own metabolism, so less of the toxic metabolite forms. Carbon tetrachloride is metabolized by the body to something even nastier. With repeated exposure, carbon tetrachloride inhibits the enzymes that do its own metabolism, so some of it never becomes the nastier metabolite. That partly protects against the repeated exposure. This is dispositional tolerance, based on A D M E. In class she said students were probably thinking carbon tetrachloride would induce the enzymes that do its metabolism, but no, it inhibits its own metabolism and kind of protects you, sort of. Induction of the enzymes is the expectation she named, and it is wrong for this compound. Down-regulated receptors would be reduced-responsiveness tolerance. Inducing a metal-carrying protein for kidney clearance is the cadmium example.",
"L14-044": "Cadmium produces dispositional tolerance by inducing the metal-carrying protein that helps the kidneys eliminate it. Cadmium is a metal and a common environmental contaminant. Like other metals, it is carried in the body by a metal carrier, which she called a metal carrying enzyme. When that carrier grabs cadmium, it helps eliminate it through the kidneys. In class she said that cadmium, especially certain valences of cadmium, can induce the expression of that carrier and help you clear it. So with repeated exposure more cadmium is cleared, and less reaches the site of toxicity. Inhibiting its own metabolism is the carbon tetrachloride example. Down-regulating receptors is reduced-responsiveness tolerance, not dispositional. Conversion to a less toxic valence was not described; valence matters because certain valences induce the carrier.",
"L14-045": "A medication that works less well at the same dose fits reduced responsiveness, by down-regulation of the target receptors. With repeated exposure, the target of the compound down-regulates, for instance fewer receptors, so the tissue responds less. That is the pharmacodynamic kind of tolerance. In class she said that if you give a person a medication over time and it starts to not work as well, you could be having reduced responsiveness because of a down-regulation of the receptors the medication targets. Induction of a carrier that clears the drug is the cadmium example of dispositional tolerance. Inhibition of a drug's own metabolism is the carbon tetrachloride example, also dispositional. Induction of clearing enzymes would be an A D M E change, which is dispositional, so it cannot be reduced responsiveness.",
"L14-046": "A compound that is lethal to one species and harmless to another shows selective toxicity. Toxicologists use specific terms for variation in effect. Selective toxicity means an exposure could be lethal to one species but not to another. Species differences means two species are both sensitive but differ qualitatively, quantitatively, or both, in how they respond, which raises the question of extrapolating results between species. Individual differences are within one species and come from each person's genetics. In class she said selective toxicity means an exposure could be lethal to one species but not to another, and species differences means a qualitative and or quantitative difference in the way two species respond. Species differences does not fit here, because one species is unharmed. Individual differences are within a species. Idiosyncratic means the reason is unknown.",
"L14-047": "The arsenic skin cancer illustrates species differences, which make animal results hard to apply to humans. Arsenic is found in groundwater in places around the world. Humans who drink it can develop a bizarre skin cancer on the palms of the hands and the soles of the feet. Rodents and the other animals tested do not reproduce it, so there is no animal model and the mechanism cannot be worked out. In class she said they have tried and tried to find an animal that mimics what happens to humans exposed to arsenic in water, and cannot. Had the human cases not already been seen, animal studies would have called the arsenic safe. Choosing the right animal to predict a drug's effects in humans is the same problem. It is not selective toxicity, because the rodents were not killed. No mechanism, such as rodents making a safer form, was given. It is not individual genetic differences, because the difference is between species.",
"L14-048": "The explanation is most likely found in the genetics of each individual. People of one species can respond to the same medication or the same environmental exposure in different ways. These are individual differences, and the reason lies in each person's genotype. Pharmacogenomics studies these differences, and for some drugs the responsible genes are already known. In class she said the answer to why is going to be found in the genetics of each individual. Species differences do not apply, because both people are the same species. Selective toxicity also compares species: a compound lethal to one species and harmless to another. Tolerance comes from repeated exposure, so it does not explain why two people differ on their first exposure.",
"L14-049": "Toxic endpoints may be described as on-target or off-target, organ specific or non-organ specific, idiosyncratic, and immunologically based, such as allergic reactions. On-target or off-target asks whether the drug is hitting the receptor it was designed for. Organ specific or not asks whether the damage is tied to one organ or whether the same pathology appears in many. Fibrosis, genotoxicity, cancer and teratogenesis are non-organ specific; the lungs, nervous system, liver and kidney are the organ-specific endpoints. Idiosyncratic, in her words, means we do not know why it is happening; it is unexplainable. The immunological category focuses mostly on allergic reactions. One exposure can have several responses, and a different mechanism may cause each. In class she said toxic endpoints may be defined as on or off target, and may be non-organ specific, organ specific or idiosyncratic. Hormetic does not belong. Hormesis is a dose-response shape, not a category of toxic endpoint.",
"L14-050": "The left half shows the intended tissue, and the right half shows an unintended tissue. The figure comes from the pharmacology textbook chapter on drug toxicity. The left is where we want the drug to act. The right is a tissue the drug may also reach, where we do not want it to act, so activity there could be a side effect. On both sides the drug, or a metabolite of it, finds receptors. On-target means it finds the receptor it was designed for. Off-target means it finds other receptors. Either can occur in either tissue. In class she said the left is the intended tissue, where we want the drug to act in the body, and the right is what is going on in an unintended tissue. The reversed pairing puts the tissues on the wrong sides. Parent drug and metabolite is wrong, because drug or metabolite activity appears on both sides. On-target and off-target is wrong, because both appear in both halves.",
"L14-051": "A drug hitting its intended receptor in a tissue where that action is unwanted is called on-target. It is still on-target because the drug finds the receptor it was meant to hit; only the tissue is wrong. In class she said that even though the drug is in the wrong tissue, when it finds the receptor we wanted it to find, it is still called on target, and that this is a part of the toxicology vernacular she really does not like. She wishes it were called off target. Off-target, in the standard vocabulary, means the drug finds other receptors it was not intended to find, either in the intended tissue or in another one. Idiosyncratic means we do not know why it happens, and here the mechanism is known. Non-organ specific describes endpoints with the same pathology in many organs, such as fibrosis.",
"L14-052": "The most common reason is that the dose at the receptor is too high. When a drug causes an adverse effect at its intended receptor in its intended tissue, it is an on-target effect. In class she said that most of the time, if you are having an on-target adverse effect in the right tissue, your dose is too high. Too much dose at the receptor can come from a deliberate overdose, an accidental dosing error by a healthcare provider, a change in pharmacokinetics such as a diseased liver that cannot clear a normal dose, or a change in receptor number. The other explanation she listed is chronic activation or inhibition of other effects. An enantiomer hitting another receptor is the thalidomide example, an off-target effect. Reaching the receptor in the wrong tissue is the diphenhydramine example, which the stem rules out by placing the effect in the intended tissue. Blocking an unintended receptor subtype, as propranolol does at beta two receptors in the airway, is off-target.",
"L14-053": "The dose at the receptor could have been raised by liver disease that slows clearance, by a change in receptor number at the target, by an accidental dosing error, or by a deliberate overdose. These are the on-target examples of too much dose at the receptor. Liver disease is a change in pharmacokinetics: the liver no longer metabolizes and clears the drug, so the normal dose gives blood concentrations that are too high. A healthcare provider's dosing error, or a patient taking too much on purpose, puts more drug than intended at the intended receptor. For the change in receptor number, she said that if receptor numbers down-regulate and the dose is kept the same, you may be overhitting those receptors. When she taught tolerance, though, down-regulation lowered the response, so the safer wording is simply a change in receptor number. The drug finding a different receptor subtype does not belong. That is off-target, not a rise in dose at the intended receptor.",
"L14-054": "Diphenhydramine's drowsiness is an on-target effect in the wrong tissue. Diphenhydramine, brand name Benadryl, is a first-generation antihistamine. It prevents allergic symptoms by hitting histamine receptors in the periphery. It also crosses into the brain and hits the same histamine receptors there, causing drowsiness. In class she said it causes drowsiness because it is hitting the histamine receptors in the brain, so that is an on-target adverse effect in the wrong tissue. Newer antihistamines such as cetirizine and fexofenadine were tweaked so they do not cross the blood-brain barrier, so they lack the drowsiness. Off-target in the wrong tissue is wrong, because the receptor is the intended one. An off-target subtype effect means hitting a different subtype, as propranolol does at beta two receptors. Too high a dose is wrong, because the drowsiness comes from where the drug acts, the brain.",
"L14-055": "Terfenadine's desired effect was peripheral H one receptor antagonism and its undesired effect was inhibition of cardiac potassium channels. The antihistamine effect came at peripheral H one receptors; the arrhythmias came from an unintended receptor, the cardiac potassium channels, so it is an off-target effect, and the drug is no longer on the market. In her words, there were cardiac problems with that drug because it was inhibiting cardiac potassium channels, and higher levels of Seldane in her blood blocked even more of her potassium channels. The tempting answer has the same two effects reversed, with the potassium channels as the desired target. H one antagonism in the brain with drowsiness is the diphenhydramine on-target example, and beta two block with asthma is the propranolol example.",
"L14-056": "The R enantiomer of thalidomide treats nausea and the S enantiomer is a potent teratogen. The drug was taken as a racemic mixture, a mix of both mirror-image forms, so people got both enantiomers. This is an off-target effect: an enantiomer hits an unintended receptor. Thalidomide was never officially on the market in the United States in the nineteen fifties, and it is used today in oncology. In class she said it is the R enantiomer that was good at helping with nausea, but the S enantiomer was a very potent teratogen. The tempting answer swaps the two, making S the helpful one and R the teratogen; that is reversed. The pure R form was not what people took, and the two enantiomers do not share both actions.",
"L14-057": "The asthma exacerbation is an off-target effect at an unintended receptor subtype, the beta two receptors in airway smooth muscle. Propranolol is meant to block beta one adrenergic receptors in the heart, which lowers heart rate. It also blocks beta two adrenergic receptors, a different subtype, and in the airway that block can narrow the airways and worsen asthma. In class she said the beta-blocker example is about subtypes of a receptor, beta one versus beta two. The tempting answer is beta one in the wrong tissue. But the airway effect comes from beta two, a subtype the drug was not meant to hit, so it is off-target, not on-target in the wrong tissue. An enantiomer hitting another receptor is the thalidomide example. Potassium channel block in the heart is the terfenadine example.",
"L14-058": "Medicinal chemists change the part of the molecule that hits the other receptor. A drug is off-target, or unclean in her word, when some molecular aspect of it interacts with receptors the designers did not know about. Once a class of drugs is therapeutically useful but shows many side effects, people in the lab go back to the molecule, identify the part hitting a different receptor, and change it so the next drug no longer hits the off-target receptors. In class she said this happens a lot. The newer antihistamines that do not cross the blood-brain barrier show the same strategy applied to an on-target, wrong-tissue effect. Raising the dose would increase both the therapeutic effect and the side effects. Giving a racemic mixture can add an unwanted enantiomer, as with thalidomide. Off-target effects have a known mechanism, another receptor, so they are not idiosyncratic.",
"L14-059": "Fibrosis causes loss of organ function through deposits of collagen and extracellular matrix proteins that stiffen the tissue. Some compounds cause the body to lay down collagen and other extracellular matrix proteins in a tissue, creating fibrotic tissue. Fibrotic tissue is stiff. The lungs can no longer flex, and in the liver the deposits get in the way of the bile ducts and hepatocyte function. Organ function is lost as working tissue is destroyed. In class she said some compounds can cause your body to lay down collagen and other extracellular matrix proteins in various tissues, and that fibrotic tissue is stiff. Death of the whole organ at once after one exposure is wrong; function is lost as stiff tissue replaces working tissue. Transformation into cancer cells is cancer, a separate non-organ-specific endpoint. Mutations in germ cells that pass to offspring describe mutagenicity.",
"L14-060": "The classic causes of pulmonary fibrosis are amiodarone, bleomycin and paraquat. Amiodarone is a cardiac medication, an antiarrhythmic, and it is also known to cause liver fibrosis. Bleomycin is a chemotherapy drug. Paraquat is an herbicide that people used to use in suicide attempts. Death from its pulmonary fibrosis is long and slow, as the lungs eventually cannot inhale and exhale. Pulmonary fibrosis occurs in the alveolar interstitium. In class she named amiodarone, bleomycin and paraquat as classic causes of pulmonary fibrosis, and named ethanol as the familiar cause of liver fibrosis, called cirrhosis. Ethanol does not belong, because its fibrosis is in the liver. Carbon tetrachloride does not belong either. It is her dispositional tolerance example, and its fibrosis is in the liver, not the lung.",
"L14-061": "Fibrosis is non-organ specific because the same collagen and extracellular matrix proteins are laid down in each organ. If you look at the cells and the chemistry of fibrotic tissue, lung fibrosis and liver fibrosis look the same, so the same pathology creates both. That holds even though particular compounds are known for particular organs. In class she said it is not like pulmonary fibrosis looks one way and liver fibrosis looks another; you see the same collagen and the same extracellular matrix proteins. The same compounds do not cause both forms. Bleomycin and paraquat are lung examples, ethanol is the liver example, and amiodarone was the one named for both. Non-organ specific does not mean fibrosis spreads from one organ to others. A different pathology in each organ is the reverse of her point.",
"L14-062": "Mutagenicity means the genetic damage is transmissible to offspring, while genotoxicity is any damage to D N A. Genotoxicity is the broad term. It covers D N A damage in somatic cells, the ordinary body cells, as well as in germ cells, the eggs and sperm. Mutagenicity is narrower. If the mutation happens in germ cells, it can be passed down, and the agent is a mutagen. In class she said that genotoxicity is damage to your D N A in general, and mutagenicity refers to the fact that it is transmissible. The reversed pairing is the tempting answer. Mutagenicity is not confined to somatic cells, because passing a change to offspring requires the germ cells. Cancer and birth defects are not split between the two terms. Both, along with genetically based disorders, are possible results of genotoxicity.",
"L14-063": "The results of genotoxicity can include cancer, teratogenesis, and genetically based disorders. Genotoxicity is damage to D N A in general. Because the damage is to the genetic material rather than to one organ, it is a non-organ-specific endpoint. Teratogenesis means birth defects. Some metabolic diseases have been traced to genetic damage, which is her example of a genetically based disorder. In class she said that the results of genotoxicity can be cancer, teratogenesis, which is birth defects, or genetically based disorders. Fibrosis does not belong. It is a separate non-organ-specific endpoint, caused by laying down collagen and matrix proteins, not by D N A damage. Allergic reactions do not belong either. They are immunologically based endpoints.",
"L14-064": "Lung and bronchial cancer ranks first in both men and women. Second is prostate cancer in men and breast cancer in women. The table comes from the American Cancer Society, two thousand nine, and ranks cancers by organ. In class she said the lung and bronchial cancer is highest for both men and women, then prostate for men and breast for women, and that most other cancers occur at about the same frequency in both sexes. The table sets up a puzzle. Cancer clearly occurs more in some organs, yet it is called non-organ specific, because cancers in different organs can share one cellular mechanism. Colon cancer came up only as the example of a mutation shared with some lung cancers, so it is not first or second. Liver tumors were the hamster example, not a top-ranked human cancer.",
"L14-065": "Cancer is non-organ specific because the same cellular mechanism can cause cancers in different organs. The incidence table shows cancer can happen in any organ, more often in some than in others. But when the cellular nature of different tumors is examined, the pathology causing a cancer in one organ can be the same as in another. Her classic example was that some lung cancers are caused by the same genetic mutation seen in colon cancers, and some lung cancers share what is seen in some breast cancers. In class she said that at the cellular level the pathology is the same, and that it is not always the case, but we do see this happening. Equal rates in every organ after adjusting for age is wrong, because the table shows rates differ by organ. Spread from one organ to others is not what non-organ specific means. A mechanism unique to each organ is the reverse of her point.",
"L14-066": "The multistage model of carcinogenesis holds that cancer usually requires more than one mutation. It would be rare for a single mutation to go all the way to cancer. The stages of the model are initiation, promotion and progression. In class she said that we think it takes more than one mutation to end up with somebody having cancer, and that it would be rare to have just one mutation going all the way to cancer. The tempting answer is a single mutation in one gene; but the model exists precisely to explain why one mutation is rarely enough. Exposure during gestation is the teratogenesis story, and a mutation in a germ cell is what makes an agent a mutagen, not what the multistage model requires.",
"L14-067": "The hamster study shows that the time of exposure during gestation determines the type of birth defect. Retinoids were well known to cause limb deformations. When the mothers were dosed at different times in gestation, other defects appeared: microphthalmia, meaning little eyes, exencephaly, spina bifida with a little later exposure, cleft palate at two points on the timeline, forelimb shortening, and effects on the tail. In class she said these other birth defects were not expected until the retinoid was given to the mothers at different times in their gestation, a classic example of why a teratogen is not necessarily organ specific. Limbs damaged whatever the day was the expectation the study overturned. Dose was not what changed the type of defect; timing was. Different compounds causing one defect is the palate table, and this study used one compound.",
"L14-068": "The point is that different compounds can cause the same defect, with the fetus sensitive to each at a different time of gestation. The table lists the gestation day of peak palate deformity in hamsters: methanol on day seven, T C D D on day eleven, two four five trichloroacetic acid on day twelve, and dexamethasone on day thirteen. That is four compounds, four peak days, and one defect, cleft palate. In class she said that methanol at seven days of gestation and T C D D at eleven days both give the cleft palate, showing that different compounds can cause the same teratogenic effect. A shared mechanism was not claimed; the table says nothing about how each acts. One compound causing different defects by day is the retinoid study. The palate is not sensitive to both on the same day, because each compound has its own peak day.",
"L14-069": "For a teratogen, the mother must take in enough for the compound to pass through to the baby. Fetal exposure comes through the mother's exposure and the compound's passage to the fetus. The fetus is exposed through the mother, so fetal exposure depends on the mother's absorption, distribution, metabolism and excretion and on whether the compound crosses the placenta. In her words, dose in teratogenicity means the mother has to get enough of the compound for it to pass through into the baby. The tempting answer is the day of gestation; timing sets the type of defect, but dose sets whether enough reaches the fetus. The fetus is not dosed directly, and the father's exposure before conception is not the route she described.",
"L14-070": "The labeling now carries risks to the fetus of use during pregnancy, effects on fertility including male fertility, and risks to a nursing baby of use while breastfeeding. The Food and Drug Administration used to rank drugs on a scale of how likely they were to cause birth defects. The Pregnancy and Lactation Labeling Rule of two thousand fourteen kept the pregnancy information and added two kinds. Fertility is new, because a drug may lower sperm count or viability. Lactation is new, because a baby should not take in certain drugs through breast milk. In class she said the labeling now includes fertility, especially male fertility, and whether lactating women should take the medication. Effects of the drug when given directly to a child do not belong. The rule covers pregnancy, fertility and lactation, not pediatric dosing.",
"L15-001": "The lungs are susceptible because of their oxidative burden, because the water solubility of an inhaled gas sets where it deposits, and because particle size and breathing pattern set where particles deposit. The oxidative burden comes from inhaled oxygen and oxygen-containing pollutant gases, plus the reactive oxygen species released by white blood cells that respond to irritants or pathogens. For a gas, its chemistry, its water solubility, decides how far down the airway it goes. For particles, size and shape decide where they settle, heavier breathing delivers more, and clearance by coughing and the cilia decides what stays. In class she listed oxidative burden, the chemistry of the gases, and particle size, shape and breathing pattern. Resident Kupffer cells that release inflammatory cytokines do not belong. They are the liver's own white blood cells, a reason the liver is susceptible. Arterioles that are highly sensitive to vasoconstrictors do not belong either. That is the kidney's reason.",
"L15-002": "The two sources are inhaled reactive gases and white blood cells responding to injury. Oxygen itself and pollutant gases with oxygen in the molecule, such as sulfur dioxide and nitrogen oxides, become very reactive as they are metabolized in the lung. White blood cells drawn in by irritants or pathogens, fighting the pathogen or cleaning up damaged tissue, release reactive oxygen species while they work. The free electrons then damage the fats and proteins of lung cells. In class she said that breathing in oxygen and other reactive compounds, plus the activity of the white blood cells, gives the lung a big oxidative burden. Inhaled particles and the cilia that clear them are a separate reason for lung susceptibility. Liver metabolites in the blood are the kidney's exposure, and surfactant is not a source. Low oxygen tension is wrong too. The problem is too much reactive oxygen, and hemoglobin picks oxygen up in the lung rather than adding to the burden.",
"L15-003": "The property that sets how far down the airway a gas travels is its water solubility. For gas exposures, water solubility determines the site of deposition, and in class she said the chemistry of the gas will determine where in the lungs that gas goes. Her direction was that very hydrophilic gases go quite far down into the lungs, and hydrophobic gases do not. Because the depth reached depends on the chemistry, the location of the injury in a damaged lung hints at what kind of gas was breathed when no one knows the exposure. Particle size is the tempting answer, but size governs where particles settle, not where a gas goes. Molecular weight does not set a gas's path, and neither does the temperature of the inhaled gas.",
"L15-004": "Medium particles are the most concerning, because they turn the airway corners and settle in the lower lung. They are heavy enough not to be breathed back out with every breath, and small enough to go around the curves of the bronchioles into the lower lung. At the still moment between breathing in and breathing out, they fall out of the air and are left behind. In class she said the medium sized particles are the ones that are the most concerning. Very small particles are so light that they do not get past the nose and upper throat, and they are breathed back out, so they do not settle in the alveoli. Large particles come in fast and cannot turn the corners where the bronchioles branch. They impact at those bifurcations in the upper airway and stick there, so speed does not carry them deeper, and they do not go on to the alveoli. Particles also carry chemicals, such as metals or organic compounds, so where they land is where the damage is.",
"L15-005": "Asbestos reaches the outer lining of the lung because of its long, thin shape. Shape matters as well as size. Long, thin particles embed in the upper airways where the lung branches. Asbestos fibers are also sharp, and some can move through the lung tissue to its outer lining, where they cause mesothelioma. In class she said that long thin particles become embedded in the upper airways as the lungs branch, and that some of those particles can move through tissues, as with mesothelioma after asbestos exposure. Water solubility is the tempting answer, but water solubility sets where a gas goes, and asbestos is a particle. Medium size explains which particles settle in the lower lung, not how fibers travel through tissue to the lining. Metal content is not the reason either. The shape is.",
"L15-006": "Jogging in a park is better because heavier breathing deposits more of the exhaust in the lungs. Breathing pattern is one of the things that set how much of an inhaled exposure reaches the lung. A walker breathes lightly. A jogger breathes much more heavily, so running beside traffic means taking in much more exhaust every minute. In class she said that walking does not make you breathe as heavily as jogging, so she suggests jogging in a park rather than beside a road. Slowed clearance by the cilia is not what changes between walking and jogging. Large particles are not carried past the airway branches by fast airflow; they come in fast and hit the branch points. Oxidative burden is a reason every lung is susceptible, but the reason she gave for the park was the breathing pattern.",
"L15-007": "The chronic responses to pulmonary exposures are fibrosis, emphysema and asthma. Fibrosis is an increase in collagen that reduces the plasticity of the lung. In emphysema the lungs become larger and too plastic, with hyperinflation, and gas exchange is reduced by loss of tissue and air trapping. Asthma is counted as a chronic response. In class she said that airway reactivity and pulmonary edema are acute, and that fibrosis, emphysema and asthma are chronic results of exposures. The two that do not belong are the acute responses. Airway reactivity is bronchoconstriction that decreases airway diameter and restricts airflow, with wheezing, coughing, chest tightness and dyspnea. Pulmonary edema is the acute exudative phase that thickens the alveolar-capillary barrier, even though its abnormalities may persist.",
"L15-008": "Bleomycin and cyclophosphamide show that pulmonary toxicity can follow a systemic exposure, not only an inhaled one. Most pulmonary toxins are airborne, such as particulates and oxygen. But these two chemotherapy drugs are given intravenously, travel in the blood, and cause pulmonary fibrosis. The herbicides monocrotaline and paraquat, which some people have used to harm themselves, also cause pulmonary fibrosis. In class she said these chemotherapy medications are given intravenously, that they are a systemic exposure, and that they cause pulmonary fibrosis, a form of pulmonary toxicity. Particle size governs where inhaled particles deposit, not where drugs arriving in the blood go. Reaching the lung through the blood does not make a drug an airborne toxin. And fibrosis is a chronic response, not an acute one after a single dose. Airway reactivity and pulmonary edema are the acute responses.",
"L15-009": "Bleomycin's lung damage is non-organ specific because the damage is fibrosis, which is classed by its mechanism rather than by the organ. Fibrosis, excess collagen and extracellular matrix deposited until the tissue is destroyed, is a non-organ-specific endpoint wherever it occurs, like cancer, genotoxicity and teratogenesis; in the lung it is also a form of pulmonary toxicity. In her words, because of the mechanism of how that damage occurs, it is considered non-organ specific, and both of these compounds cause fibrosis. The tempting answer is that bleomycin is inhaled as an aerosol and so is an airborne pulmonary toxin; bleomycin is given intravenously. The lungs do show organ-specific toxicity, and pulmonary edema does not spread to other organs.",
"L15-010": "The pulmonary response to fluorine or chlorine gas is acute and caustic, driven by the water in the lungs. A halogen gas is very irritating, definitely acute, and caustic in the wet lung, because it reacts with the water lining it. Chlorine gas releases from industrial accidents and train derailments are real examples. In her words, it would definitely be an acute response, very caustic to the lungs because of the water in our lungs, like chlorine gas or any of the halogens in the periodic table. The tempting answer is chronic fibrosis appearing years later; but fibrosis is a chronic response, and a halogen gas exposure is an acute one. Emphysema is chronic as well, and the injury is local to the lung lining, not a systemic effect after absorption.",
"L15-011": "The brain capillary is a barrier because of tight junctions between the vessel cells and tightly joined glial cells around the vessel. In a brain capillary the cells of the vessel touch tightly, so solutes cannot slip between them. Outside the vessel, glial cells are tightly connected to each other. A systemic capillary is the opposite. It has openings called fenestrations, sometimes large, and it is surrounded by pericytes that are not tightly joined, so solutes move out easily. In class she said that instead of large fenestration sites, the brain has very tight junctions, and the brain capillaries are surrounded by glial cells that are much more tightly connected. Each wrong pairing borrows a systemic feature. Fenestrations belong to systemic capillaries, not the brain. Loosely joined pericytes also belong to systemic capillaries. Fenestrations together with loose pericytes is the systemic capillary in the figure, which lets solutes out easily.",
"L15-012": "Three statements are correct. Penetration depends on a compound's lipophilicity, the barrier is not continuous, and the barrier is still developing at birth. In brain capillaries, tight junctions and tightly joined glial cells close the gaps between cells. A compound must pass through the endothelial cell membranes themselves, so lipophilic compounds cross, and larger or non-lipophilic compounds are kept out. Transporters also move some compounds back to the blood. Some parts of the brain have a looser barrier than others. For several months to years after birth the barrier is still developing, so a drug kept out of an adult brain can enter a young child's brain. In class she said the barrier protects us especially from non-lipophilic or larger compounds, and that it is still developing at birth. The two that do not belong say the reverse. Larger compounds do not cross more easily, and lipophilic compounds are the ones that get through, not the ones kept out.",
"L15-013": "The feature that explains harm to newborns is that the blood-brain barrier is still developing at birth. It stays immature for several months to years, so a drug that is kept out of the adult brain can cross into a young child's brain. In class she said that some drugs given to a very young child crossed the blood-brain barrier and caused harm, while the same medication was not an issue for adults, whose barrier was tight and contained. Pediatric dosing has to allow for an immature barrier. The barrier is not tighter in newborns. It is the reverse, less complete. The barrier does not keep lipophilic drugs out of the adult brain. Lipophilic drugs cross, and the barrier keeps out larger and non-lipophilic ones. Transporters do not pump drugs into the newborn brain. They move some compounds from the brain back to the blood.",
"L15-014": "The nervous system is susceptible because of four features. Neurons have very high energy needs and depend on aerobic metabolism, so anything that cuts oxygen or energy causes trouble. Axons are very long, and nutrients and waste must be transported up and down them. Myelin is found in no other organ. It speeds conduction, but the cells that make it can be damaged. Those are Schwann cells in peripheral nerves and oligodendrocytes in the brain and spinal cord. When myelin is damaged, conduction slows. The nervous system also depends on neurotransmission, and release, binding or reuptake of a transmitter can each be disturbed. In class she listed high energy requirements, long axons, the myelin sheath, and neurotransmission. Resident immune cells that release reactive oxygen species do not belong. That describes the liver's Kupffer cells and part of the lung's oxidative burden.",
"L15-015": "Tingling of the hands and feet during metronidazole treatment is myelinopathy. Metronidazole, also called Flagyl, damages the cells that make myelin. Myelin speeds conduction along the nerve, so when it is damaged, conduction slows, and the classic symptom is tingling of the hands and feet. This was her own story. On a second, higher-dose course for Giardia, her hands and feet tingled, and she recognized that the drug causes myelinopathy and took herself off it. Remyelination can happen, but it takes a long time. Neuronopathy is death of the entire neuron. Axonopathy is degeneration of the axon while the cell body survives. Interruption of neurotransmission is the organophosphate pattern. Metronidazole is her example of myelinopathy, and organophosphates are her example of disturbed neurotransmission.",
"L15-016": "Neuronopathy is death of the entire neuron. Axonopathy is degeneration of the axon while the cell body survives. Myelinopathy is disruption of myelin or injury to the myelinating cells. Interruption of neurotransmission is excitation blocked or stimulation excessive, with no cell death. In the figure, neuron A is normal, B shows neuronopathy, C axonopathy, D myelinopathy, and E disturbed neurotransmission. In neuronopathy, astrocytes often multiply around the lost neuron, which is gliosis. In axonopathy, the surviving cell body shows chromatolysis. In class she asked what the toxin did to neuron B and answered that it killed the whole thing. She called C axonopathy and D myelinopathy, and said that in E the neurotransmission is being disturbed. A given neurotoxin usually causes one of these discrete patterns. The pair most easily confused is neuronopathy and axonopathy. When the whole cell dies, it is neuronopathy. When only the axon degenerates and the cell body survives, it is axonopathy.",
"L15-017": "Organophosphate pesticides produce interruption of neurotransmission, with excess neurotransmitter in the synaptic cleft. They block the enzyme that breaks down acetylcholine, so the transmitter builds up. That is her example for neuron E: a common class of pesticides that perturbs neurotransmission. A neurotoxin typically causes one specific kind of damage; her two examples are organophosphates for neurotransmission and metronidazole for myelinopathy. In her words, organophosphates, a common class of pesticides, cause neurotransmission damage. Myelinopathy is the tempting answer, but myelin is not their target; metronidazole was her myelinopathy example. Death of the whole neuron with gliosis is neuronopathy, and axon degeneration with chromatolysis is axonopathy. It is very rare for one compound to cause more than one discrete pattern.",
"L15-018": "Interruption of neurotransmission leaves the neuron looking unchanged. The cell is intact. What is missing is the signaling between cells, so the problem is recognized by the loss of function. In class she said that neurotransmission toxicity will not change the look of the cell, but you can figure it out from the lack of neurotransmission. The other three patterns each change what the cell looks like under the microscope. In neuronopathy the whole neuron dies, and astrocytes multiply around it. In axonopathy the axon degenerates, and the surviving cell body shows chromatolysis. In myelinopathy the myelin sheath, or the cells that make it, are visibly damaged. Most neurotoxins cause one of these discrete patterns. Organophosphates, which disturb neurotransmission, are her example of the pattern that leaves the cell looking normal.",
"L15-019": "The white blood cells that reside in the liver are the Kupffer cells. They are a kind of white blood cell that waits in the liver to be called up, to clean up dead tissue or fight pathogens. While they work, they release inflammatory cytokines and reactive oxygen species, which add to the injury. Having its own inflammatory cells is one reason the liver is susceptible. Kupffer cells are found throughout the liver, not in only one zone. In class she described them as a kind of white blood cell that resides in the liver, waiting to be called up. Hepatocytes are the tempting answer, but hepatocytes are the liver cells that do much of the metabolism, not immune cells. Schwann cells make myelin in peripheral nerves. Pericytes surround the systemic capillaries outside the brain.",
"L15-020": "Resident immune cells make the liver more susceptible because, while cleaning up damaged cells, they release inflammatory cytokines and reactive oxygen species. These are the Kupffer cells. Their job is to clean up dying or dead tissue and fight pathogens. The cytokines call in more immune cells, which adds inflammation, and the reactive oxygen species add damage, on top of the injury from the toxin. In class she said the Kupffer cells are there to help, but as a side effect of their job they can create more of a mess through the inflammatory cytokines and reactive oxygen species they release. Bioactivation to toxic metabolites is done by metabolizing enzymes in the hepatocytes, such as cytochrome P four fifty two E one, not by immune cells. Taking up and concentrating xenobiotics is a separate reason the liver is susceptible, and it belongs to the liver cells generally. Constricting blood flow is a kidney vulnerability, not what Kupffer cells do.",
"L15-021": "The organ is the liver, and what increasingly explains the reaction is a genetic variant that changes how the drug is metabolized. An idiosyncratic reaction is a strong, unexpected reaction in one person that most people do not have. The liver is the organ most likely to have these reactions because it does most of the body's drug metabolism. A person with a rare variant may metabolize the drug differently, perhaps to a hepatotoxic metabolite. In class she said the liver tends to have that kind of reaction and the kidneys don't really do that, and that pharmacogenomics is now explaining reactions once called unexplainable. The kidney's sensitivity to vasoconstrictors is real, but the kidneys do not tend to have idiosyncratic reactions. Oxidative burden affects every lung and does not explain one person's reaction. The liver because of its large size is the trap. The organ is right, but she said size has nothing to do with it.",
"L15-022": "Blood comes in by the hepatic artery and the portal vein and leaves by the central vein. The two inflow vessels sit at the edges of each lobule, where the bile ducts also begin. Blood trickles from the edges down along the cords of hepatocytes and drains into the central vein in the middle. In class she said that blood comes into each lobe through the hepatic artery and the hepatic portal vein, trickles across the cords of hepatocytes, and drains out through the central vein. The portal vein brings blood from the gut, which is why an oral dose meets the liver first. This direction of flow is the basis of the three zones, with zone one at the inflow and zone three at the central vein. The options that bring blood in by the central vein reverse the flow. The bile duct carries bile, not blood.",
"L15-023": "The table shows that most compounds cause one discrete kind of liver injury, and ethanol is unusual in causing many. Ethanol appears under hepatocyte death, fatty liver, also called steatosis, inflammation, liver regeneration, and fibrosis. Aflatoxin, a chemical made by molds that grow on grains, appears only under liver cancer, and it carries a very high risk for liver cancer. In class she said other compounds are not so good at hitting many different parts of the liver, and that aflatoxin really is only causing liver cancer. She also said the table does not have to be memorized, but each compound is usually discrete in the kind of hepatotoxicity it causes. Potency is not what the table shows. Breadth of response is. Liver cancer is not the usual endpoint of every hepatotoxin. Acetaminophen, for example, causes hepatocyte death. And aflatoxin is not low risk.",
"L15-024": "Hepatocyte death is death of the hepatocytes, the cells that do much of the liver's metabolism. Acetaminophen and ethanol are examples. Fatty liver is also called steatosis, and ethanol causes it. Canalicular cholestasis means the bile duct is blocked. The cells lining the bile ducts are damaged and inflamed, so the duct narrows. Liver cancer is the one response aflatoxin produces on the table. The other responses listed are bile duct damage, sinusoidal endothelial damage, inflammation, liver regeneration, and fibrosis and cirrhosis. In class she said hepatocyte death is the actual death of the hepatocytes, the ones that do a lot of the metabolism, and that canalicular cholestasis means the bile duct gets blocked and narrowed because the cells lining it are damaged and inflamed. The pair most easily confused is hepatocyte death and steatosis. Dead metabolizing cells is hepatocyte death. Fat building up in the liver is steatosis.",
"L15-025": "The pattern of liver injury is found by imaging and a liver biopsy, and the pattern gives a hint about the exposure. Different hepatotoxins cause different, discrete kinds of damage. So when liver function is falling and no one knows the exposure, the pattern of injury points toward what the patient was exposed to. A punch biopsy is done externally with a needle, and even a small core of tissue is enough to start working it out. In class she said they often do imaging and a liver biopsy to look for the pattern of injury, and that pattern gives a hint of what the patient might have been exposed to. Liver function tests show that the liver is affected, but the pattern takes imaging and biopsy, and it hints at the exposure rather than naming the toxin. Serum creatinine is a kidney measure. Genotyping can explain an idiosyncratic reaction, but it does not show the pattern of injury.",
"L15-026": "Zone three is next to the central vein, where blood leaves the liver. The acinus divides the liver by the path of the blood. Zone one is closest to the inflow vessels, the hepatic artery and the portal vein. Zone two is intermediate. Zone three is closest to the central venule drainage. In class she said zone one is closest to the vessels where the blood comes into the liver, and zone three is closest to the vessel where the blood leaves. Because zone three gets the blood last, it is the low-oxygen zone, and it is also where cytochrome P four fifty two E one is most highly expressed. The zone next to the hepatic artery and portal vein is zone one. The region between the inflow vessels and the central vein is zone two. The region with the highest oxygen content is zone one, not zone three.",
"L15-027": "Blood in zone three is relatively hypoxic because the hepatocytes upstream take oxygen out of it as it flows. Blood enters from the hepatic artery and portal vein relatively oxygenated. As it trickles past the cords of hepatocytes, they grab the oxygen. By the time it drains out through the central vein, it is relatively hypoxic. That makes an oxygen gradient across the acinus, high in zone one and low in zone three, and the gradient is one reason different hepatotoxins injure different zones. In class she said the point is that blood comes into the liver relatively oxygenated and leaves through the central vein relatively hypoxic. The central vein does not bring blood in. It is the outflow, and the gradient forms inside the liver. Kupffer cells are found throughout the liver, not concentrated in zone three. And both inflow vessels enter next to zone one, so zone three is not fed by the portal vein alone.",
"L15-028": "Hepatocyte death from an acetaminophen overdose is in zone three, because cytochrome P four fifty two E one, which makes N A P Q I, is most highly expressed there. In an overdose this enzyme bioactivates acetaminophen to N A P Q I, the hepatotoxic metabolite. The metabolite is made where the enzyme is, so the hepatocytes in zone three make the most and are the ones that die. In class she worked it as a little case. The acetaminophen is metabolized by two E one, two E one is most highly expressed in zone three, so on biopsy zone three shows hepatocyte death. The zone of injury follows the bioactivating enzyme, not the order in which blood reaches the cells. So zone one, which receives the drug first, is wrong. Oxygen is high in zone one, but the enzyme is in zone three. And Kupffer cells are found throughout the liver, so zone two having the fewest is false.",
"L15-029": "The correct statement is that many phase two enzymes sit in zone one, and cytochrome P four fifty two E one is more highly expressed in zone three. Metabolizing enzymes are not homogeneously expressed across the liver. A toxic metabolite is made where its enzyme is, so the hepatocytes in that zone take the damage. That is why acetaminophen kills zone three hepatocytes. Together with the oxygen gradient, this uneven expression explains why different hepatotoxins injure different regions. In class she said a lot of the phase two enzymes are in zone one, and some of the P four fifties are more in zone three. The reversed pairing puts each enzyme in the wrong zone. Putting both in zone three is wrong, because many phase two enzymes are in zone one. Even expression of the P four fifties across the zones is false, and two E one is her example of an enzyme concentrated in one zone.",
"L15-030": "Four features make the kidneys susceptible. The kidneys receive a large share of the blood, and much of it has just passed through the liver, carrying metabolites the liver made. As the kidney concentrates urine, it also concentrates poisons in the tubule, and the tubular cells take them up. Compounds are transported into, build up in, and are metabolized by the kidney cells, so toxicity can arise right inside them. The feature unique to the kidney is its sensitivity to vasoconstriction of the afferent and efferent arterioles, which cuts blood flow and the glomerular filtration rate. In class she said the kidney gets lots of blood that has gone through the liver, and that its cells take up and concentrate compounds like the liver does. Resident immune cells that release reactive oxygen species do not belong. That is the liver's reason, the Kupffer cells.",
"L15-031": "Vasoconstriction of the afferent and efferent arterioles reduces blood flow to the kidney and lowers the glomerular filtration rate. Sensitivity to vasoconstrictors is the kidney's unique vulnerability. Constricting or dilating these arterioles also changes the concentration of what the kidney receives. Prerenal injury and vasoconstriction are both mechanisms of chemically induced acute renal failure. In class she said the kidney is sensitive to any vasoconstriction of both the afferent and the efferent arteriole, and if you reduce that blood flow, you reduce the glomerular filtration rate. Raised glomerular pressure with a rise in filtration is wrong, because reduced flow lowers the filtration rate. Loss of the urine-concentrating mechanism is not the consequence. Concentrating urine is a separate reason for susceptibility. Allergic inflammation of the interstitium is interstitial nephritis, an immune injury, not a flow effect.",
"L15-032": "Vasoconstriction, crystalluria, tubular toxicity and interstitial nephritis are all mechanisms of chemically induced acute renal failure. The kinds of damage are sorted by mechanism. Vasoconstriction cuts blood flow to the nephron, and the non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs are her example. Crystalluria means a drug forms crystals in the urine that block the tubules, as with sulfonamides and acyclovir. Tubular toxicity is direct injury to the tubular cells, as with aminoglycosides and cisplatin. Interstitial nephritis is an inflammatory, often allergic injury, and she added the proton pump inhibitors to its list. In class she said that what matters is these different kinds of renal damage in general. Canalicular cholestasis does not belong. It is a liver response, where bile flow is blocked as the cells lining the bile canaliculi are damaged and inflamed.",
"L15-033": "The class is the non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, and patients should limit their use and drink plenty of water. On her kidney table these common drugs appear under three kinds of injury: interstitial nephritis, vasoconstriction and glomerulopathy. That is why they are the drugs she worries about most. They are taken widely, often without supervision. In class she said that she worries most about the non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, that they can cause interstitial nephritis, vasoconstriction and glomerulopathy, and that people should limit their use and drink lots of water while taking them. Proton pump inhibitors cause interstitial nephritis, and their over-the-counter label limits use to two weeks, but they are not listed for the other two injuries. Angiotensin converting enzyme inhibitors sit in the prerenal column. Aminoglycosides sit under tubular toxicity.",
"L15-034": "This is interstitial nephritis, an allergic response to the proton pump inhibitor, and the drug is stopped for good. Proton pump inhibitors such as omeprazole are not on the kidney table, but she added them under interstitial nephritis. The reaction happens especially in patients with an aspirin allergy. The allergy shows up in the kidney, not as hives or trouble breathing. This was her father's case. A geriatric patient allergic to aspirin, he took over-the-counter omeprazole for a few months, and routine blood work showed a high serum creatinine. He was told to stop and never go back on proton pump inhibitors, and he needed erythropoietin for a while. In class she said proton pump inhibitors can cause interstitial nephritis, especially in patients with an aspirin allergy. Crystalluria is wrong. These drugs do not form crystals, and the drug could not be continued. Tubular toxicity with a lower dose is wrong, because the mechanism was immune, not concentration. Prerenal injury is wrong too. It was not a flow problem, and angiotensin converting enzyme inhibitors are themselves in the prerenal column.",
"L15-035": "The two reasons are that reflux needing treatment for more than two weeks should be checked, and that proton pump inhibitors can cause interstitial nephritis. Reflux bad enough to need the drug for longer may need a diagnosis. Interstitial nephritis is an allergic injury in the kidney that does not show itself the way hives do. In her father's case, after a few months of over-the-counter omeprazole, it showed up only as a high serum creatinine on routine blood work. In class she said that if reflux is bad enough to need the drug for more than two weeks, you should get it checked, and that these drugs can cause interstitial nephritis. Crystalluria is the wrong kidney injury; proton pump inhibitors were placed under interstitial nephritis. Tolerance is not a reason the label gives.",
"L15-036": "The difference she ranked probably the most important is the local expression of metabolizing enzymes. Renal cells metabolize the compounds they take up. Which enzymes are expressed in which part of the kidney decides where a toxic metabolite is made, and that is where the injury appears. It works the way cytochrome P four fifty two E one places acetaminophen injury in zone three of the liver. In class she said probably the most important reason is all that metabolism going on in the renal cells, depending on what enzymes are expressed in what part of the kidney. Tubular toxicity and crystalluria, she said, depend more on the chemistry of the drugs. The other site-specific differences listed are blood flow, transport and accumulation of chemicals, properties of the epithelium, reactivity of targets, the balance of bioactivation and detoxification, cellular energetics, and repair mechanisms. Blood flow, energetics and repair are all on that list, but none was her lead reason.",
"L15-037": "An idiosyncratic toxic response is a genetically determined, abnormally strong reaction in one person. It is defined as genetically determined abnormal reactivity to a chemical. The response is qualitatively like other people's, but extreme. In class she said idiosyncratic means a reaction we don't always know why it's happening, and that such reactions are often pretty strong and sometimes unexpected. Pharmacogenomics is beginning to explain them, tracing some to a rare genetic variant, and the liver is the organ most likely to have them. Calling every low-frequency reaction idiosyncratic is an improper use of the term. An immune-mediated hypersensitivity reaction, types one through four, is an immunologic response, a separate category. A dose-related reaction seen in most people at high doses is ordinary toxicity, not idiosyncratic.",
"L15-038": "The two broad categories of drug-triggered immune responses are hypersensitivity and autoimmune reactions. Hypersensitivity is the allergic response, types one through four; an autoimmune reaction is the immune system turning on the patient's own body. In her words, the heading is better read as immunologic responses, with hypersensitivity, a type one, two, three or four allergic response, as the first category, and autoimmune reactions as the second. The tempting answer is hapten formation and direct activation; but those are two ways a drug can be recognized as foreign, not the two categories of response. Immediate and delayed describe timing, and idiosyncratic and organ-specific are other ways of classing toxic endpoints.",
"L15-039": "A small-molecule drug triggers an immune response by acting as a hapten bound to a protein. Small molecules, less than six hundred daltons, may get bound up by certain proteins in the body. The drug-protein complex is what the immune system recognizes as foreign and mounts a response against. In class she said those small drugs may get bound up by certain proteins in the body, creating haptens, and that drug-protein complex can trigger the immune system. She called the complex itself a hapten, while the formal definition calls the small molecule itself the hapten. Direct activation of the immune system is how large drugs, therapeutic peptides and proteins, do it. Small drugs are not too small to be recognized once they are bound to a protein. And bioactivation to a toxic metabolite in the liver explains hepatotoxicity, such as acetaminophen in zone three, not an immune response.",
"L15-040": "The therapies increasingly triggering autoimmune responses are the biologicals, the protein and peptide therapies. Large drugs like these can activate the immune system directly. The autoimmune responses they trigger do not happen to everyone, are hard to predict, and can be quite harmful. In class she said this is happening with more and more frequency as more biologicals reach the market. Small-molecule drugs acting as haptens are the tempting answer. Haptens are how small drugs, under six hundred daltons, trigger an immune response by binding a body protein, but the growing autoimmune problem she named is the biologicals. Proton pump inhibitors were her example of interstitial nephritis, an allergic injury in the kidney. Bleomycin and cyclophosphamide were her examples of systemic drugs that cause pulmonary fibrosis.",
"L15-041": "Two statements are true. A delayed response may be observed weeks to years after exposure, and immediate effects can occur with each dose in a chronic exposure. Her answer to the delayed statement was yes. Diethylstilbestrol, D E S, with vaginal cancer, and triorthocresyl phosphate, T O C P, with neurotoxicity, are the extreme examples of delayed effects. Her answer to the chronic statement was also yes. A worker who walks into a workplace with a leak day after day may develop no tolerance, and so gets drowsiness or a headache every time. The other three are false. Tissue injury is not always reversible. It depends on whether the tissue can regenerate, and central nervous system injury, cancer and birth defects are irreversible. Toxins do cause local effects, right where the body is first touched, as with chlorine gas. And systemic effects do require absorption. In class she said that to become systemic, a substance is going through the bloodstream, so it has to be absorbed.",
"L15-042": "The non-organ-specific toxic response on the list is teratogenicity. Teratogenicity, the causing of birth defects, is not tied to one organ: one teratogen can cause different defects depending on when in gestation the exposure happens. Toxic endpoints are non-organ specific, meaning fibrosis, genotoxicity, cancer and teratogenesis; organ specific, meaning nervous system, lungs, liver and kidney; idiosyncratic; or immunologic. In her words, neurotoxicity is the nerves, pulmonary toxins act in the lungs, and nephrotoxins in the kidneys, so teratogenicity is the one on this list that is non-organ specific. The tempting answers each name an organ, so all three are organ specific.",
"L15-043": "Four statements are correct. The liver expresses many enzymes that may metabolize drugs to toxic compounds, it receives a large volume of blood through the portal circulation, it takes up and concentrates xenobiotics, and it has its own immune cells. Each is a step in how a toxin reaches and harms the liver. Swallowed compounds go from the gut straight to the liver in the portal blood. The liver cells take them up and concentrate them. The liver's enzymes may bioactivate them to toxic metabolites right there. Then the Kupffer cells, cleaning up damaged cells, release inflammatory cytokines and reactive oxygen species that add to the injury. In class she said all of these were correct except that it is a large organ. Size really does not have anything to do with it. The liver is susceptible because of what it does, not how big it is.",
"L15-044": "One reason for regional susceptibility across the liver is the relatively high oxygen content in zone one. Blood enters zone one well oxygenated and loses oxygen to the hepatocytes on its way to zone three. If the enzymes that metabolize a toxin need oxygen, the high oxygen in zone one helps them work there, and if their product is hepatotoxic, zone one is injured. In class she asked whether high oxygen is in zone one or zone three, answered zone one, and said that having a lot of oxygen there helps those enzymes, and unfortunately that metabolism may produce a hepatotoxic metabolite. Homogeneous expression of P four fifties is false. Most metabolizing enzymes are not expressed evenly, and cytochrome P four fifty two E one, highest in zone three, is her example. The statement that zone two has few Kupffer cells is also false. She said Kupffer cells go everywhere in the liver, not just one area.",
"L15-045": "The blood concentration stays high because most of the drug is held in lipid, muscle or bone. A very large volume of distribution means the drug does not stay in the blood vessels. It moves out and is sequestered in fat, muscle and bone. Hemodialysis can only clear drug that is in the blood. After a round of dialysis, drug moves back out of the tissues and refills the blood. In class she said you have to wait for the drug to move out of the lipids, muscle or bone back into the blood before hemodialysis can grab it again, so these patients may need dialysis for a long time. Drugs with a large volume of distribution, over five liters per kilogram, include antidepressants, antipsychotics, antimalarials, opioids, propranolol and verapamil. Exceeded protein binding raises the free fraction but does not create a tissue reservoir. The change in an overdose runs from first order to zero order, not the reverse. Half-life tends to get longer in an overdose, not shorter.",
"L15-046": "Blood concentrations are much higher than expected because first-pass metabolism in the liver is saturated. Normally an oral dose goes to the liver, and part of it is cleared there before it reaches the circulation. In an overdose the liver does all the metabolism it can, but there is too much drug for it to handle. The excess goes right through the liver unmetabolized and into the circulation. In class she said that if you send the liver a huge amount of drug and it is doing all the work it can, the drug will go right through the liver, not be metabolized, and go into circulation. The drug does not bypass the portal vein and the liver. Oral drug still reaches the liver first, and the liver just cannot keep up. The liver bioactivating more is the opposite of the point, which is less metabolism. And plasma protein binding capacity does not rise in an overdose. It is exceeded, which raises the free fraction.",
"L15-047": "An iron overdose produces very high absorption because iron is caustic and damages the gut wall. Much gut absorption is by active transport, not diffusion, and oral iron is absorbed by an active transport that saturates quickly. That saturation is protective, so a couple of tablets deliver only so much, which matters because free iron in the blood is very damaging. In an overdose, iron is so caustic that it blows apart the gut. The saturable barrier is gone, and dissolving iron goes straight into the bloodstream. This is the point that drugs may injure the gastrointestinal tract, increasing absorption. In class she said iron is very caustic, it blows apart your gut, and then you don't have the protection of a saturable active transport. Induction of more transporters is not the mechanism. Iron does not become lipophilic and diffuse across. Saturated first pass in the liver is a different kinetic change. Iron's problem is damage to the gut.",
"L15-048": "The free fraction rises, because plasma protein binding capacity is exceeded. A big dose of a highly protein-bound drug saturates the plasma proteins. More drug then circulates unbound, and free drug is what acts, so the risk of toxicity rises. She qualified the wording to mean blood protein binding. In class she said that with a big dose there is more drug free in the blood, not bound to the plasma proteins, so there is a risk of toxicity because the plasma proteins are oversaturated. The free fraction does not fall. The body does not make more plasma protein to meet the dose. It is not unchanged either. The bound percentage holds only while capacity is not exceeded. And the answer that it rises because the liver releases stored drug has the right direction but the wrong reason.",
"L15-049": "At the very high plasma concentrations of an overdose, elimination may change to zero order, which extends the half-life. In first-order elimination, a fixed fraction of the drug is cleared in each unit of time. When the elimination pathways are saturated, only a fixed amount can be cleared in each unit of time, which is zero order. So the drug stays in the body longer than its usual half-life would predict. Like saturated first-pass metabolism and exceeded protein binding, it is one way an overdose keeps more drug around for longer. Zero order with a shorter half-life has the direction wrong. Second order is not the change that occurs. First order with a shorter half-life misses the change entirely.",
"L15-050": "The figure shows that drug B can be dosed higher than drug A without reaching coma. The figure plots central nervous system effect, from sedation to hypnosis to anesthesia to coma, against increasing dose. Both drugs share a mechanism and sedate at the same dose, so at the therapeutic endpoint they look alike. In overdose they differ. Drug A rises in a straight line to coma. Drug B levels off at anesthesia. So A is the more dangerous drug in overdose. In class she asked which one you can increase the dose of and not have an unwanted adverse effect, and the answer was B. She explained it as a different therapeutic index, and her teaching point is that two medications may have the same therapeutic index but unequal safe dosing. What matters is the overlap of the therapeutic and toxic response curves. The reversed answer is wrong, because A is the one that reaches coma. They do not behave alike from sedation through coma. And B can be dosed above sedation without an unwanted effect.",
"L15-051": "A tricyclic antidepressant overdose is so dangerous because the drug reduces sweating while increasing muscle activity and the potential for seizures. At a toxic dose a drug can add pharmacodynamic effects that are not part of therapy. Muscle activity and seizures build heat, and sweating would help shed it, but the drug blocks sweating. She called it a double whammy. She also said tricyclic overdoses are horrible to treat, and it is rare to save the patient. In class she said the tricyclics reduce sweating in overdose, at the same time that they are increasing muscular activity and potential for seizures. Reduced blood flow to the kidney and liver, slowing elimination, is the beta-blocker and calcium channel blocker example. Increased sweating with reduced muscle activity reverses both effects. Saturated plasma protein binding is a kinetic change, not the pharmacodynamic one.",
"L15-052": "The effect that keeps the drug level high is reduced blood flow to the kidney and the liver, which slows elimination. Beta-blockers and calcium channel blockers lower heart rate and blood pressure. In an overdose they also reduce blood flow to the kidneys and the liver, the organs that eliminate the drug. So the body cannot clear the drug well, and the level stays high. The pharmacodynamic effect and the kinetic problem feed on each other. In class she said that by reducing blood flow to the kidneys and the liver you reduce the body's ability to eliminate the drug, and the dose stays high. Reduced sweating with more muscle activity and seizures is the tricyclic antidepressant example. Saturated protein binding is a kinetic change, not this pharmacodynamic one. No enzyme induction or toxic metabolite is involved.",
"L15-053": "The three basic approaches are toxicokinetic-based treatment, inactivation of the poison, and pharmacologically based treatment. Toxicokinetic treatment changes what happens to the poison in the body. Inactivation binds the poison so it cannot act. Pharmacologically based treatment counters what the poison does. In class she said we treat either based on kinetics, by trying to inactivate the poison, or based on the pharmacology, the pharmacodynamics of what's going on. Prevention of absorption, enhancement of metabolism and enhancement of elimination are methods within one approach, the toxicokinetic one, which has four methods. Receptor antagonists, receptor agonists and bypassing an inhibited pathway are categories within the pharmacological approach, which has four categories. Emesis, gastric lavage and activated charcoal are methods within one toxicokinetic method, prevention of absorption.",
"L15-054": "The toxicokinetic methods are prevention of absorption, inhibition of toxication, enhancement of metabolism, and enhancement of elimination. Each works on what the body does with the poison, to lower the amount in the body. Preventing absorption keeps it from getting in, as with activated charcoal. Inhibiting toxication stops bioactivation to a nastier compound, as when fomepizole or ethanol is given for methanol. Enhancing metabolism speeds detoxification, as when sodium thiosulfate is given so the body's own enzyme, rhodanese, can turn cyanide into thiocyanate. It is not done by inducing enzymes. Enhancing elimination gets the poison out faster, as with ion trapping in the urine. In class she called all four kinetically based treatments. Inactivation of the poison does not belong. It is a separate approach, such as binding a metal with a chelator.",
"L15-055": "Emesis, whole bowel irrigation, and multiple-dose activated charcoal are methods to prevent absorption of an ingested poison. Each acts while the poison is still in the gut. Emesis brings it back up from the stomach, though it is no longer used because of the risk of aspiration. Whole bowel irrigation flushes the whole gut with a polyethylene glycol solution, as for a colonoscopy. Activated charcoal binds the poison so it leaves in the feces, and repeated doses also catch drug that the liver sends back into the intestine in bile. Gastric lavage and single-dose charcoal belong on the same list. Hemodialysis does not belong. It removes poison that has already been absorbed into the blood, so it enhances elimination. Ion trapping with sodium bicarbonate does not belong either. It traps a weak acid in the urine and so enhances renal elimination.",
"L15-056": "Syrup of ipecac is no longer sold or used, and the biggest risk of induced emesis is aspiration. Ipecac makes a person vomit. While vomiting, stomach contents can be breathed into the lungs, which is aspiration. Because of that risk, ipecac is no longer sold in the United States and is not used in hospitals, and emesis is no longer a first-line treatment. In class she said they no longer even sell it in the U S, they do not use it in hospitals because it is too risky, and aspiration is the biggest risk. Still being used in emergency rooms is wrong on status, even though aspiration is the right risk. Esophageal rupture is the concern with gastric lavage, not emesis. Ipecac is not sold over the counter, and dehydration is not the risk she named.",
"L15-057": "The correct statement is that large tablets may not come back up the tube. In gastric lavage a tube is passed down to the stomach, fluid is pumped in, and then it is sucked back out. The esophagus is not that big, so big tablets are hard to pump back out through the tube. The other risk is that the tube can go into the lungs and fluid can be pumped into them. In class she said to put the tube down and hope it doesn't go into the lungs, and that lavage should not be done for anyone with an esophageal rupture or problems with the stomach, because it is dangerous. So it is not safe with an esophageal rupture. Flushing the whole gut with polyethylene glycol is whole bowel irrigation, not lavage, which washes the stomach only. And lavage does not work best hours later. The time window she gave was for single-dose charcoal, one to two hours, and lavage works only on what is still in the stomach.",
"L15-058": "Whole bowel irrigation is the same procedure as colonoscopy preparation. The patient drinks a polyethylene glycol electrolyte solution, such as GoLYTELY, until it passes through, and the whole gut is flushed from the stomach to the rectum. The gut must be open, with no ileus and no blockage. It is messy, but for a massive, dangerous ingestion an experienced emergency team may try it. In class she said that a colonoscopy prep is whole bowel irrigation. Pumping fluid into the stomach through a tube and back out is gastric lavage, which washes only the stomach. A single dose of activated charcoal binds the poison rather than flushing it out. Vomiting with ipecac empties the stomach upward, and it is no longer used.",
"L15-059": "The correct statement is that activated charcoal adsorbs certain compounds, and they leave in the feces. Activated charcoal is purified charcoal with very fine particles, given as a slurry in water. Its chemistry decides what sticks. If the poison does not stick to charcoal, charcoal is pointless. Charcoal itself is not absorbed, so the charcoal and the bound poison pass out of the body in the feces. In class she said it is purified charcoal with very fine particles, that not every compound will be grabbed by charcoal, and that what is grabbed goes out by fecal elimination. It is not absorbed into the blood with the bound drug. It is not pleasant to take. It is black and messy, and people often vomit it, which risks aspiration. It is not ordinary charcoal ground coarse.",
"L15-060": "Single-dose activated charcoal probably needs to be given within one to two hours of the ingestion. A single dose can only bind drug that is still in the stomach. Wait too long and the drug goes past the stomach and starts getting absorbed into the bloodstream, out of reach. In class she said it probably needs to be done within one to two hours of ingestion. In a later lecture she said up to two hours, adding that they used to say just an hour. Her key words the best time frame as less than sixty minutes after ingestion. Both are her words: less than sixty minutes on her key, and up to two hours in lecture. Two to four hours is too late, because the drug has passed the stomach. Six to eight hours is far too late. Twenty-four hours is far too late as well, since a single dose binds only what is still in the gut.",
"L15-061": "Multiple-dose activated charcoal is reserved for a compound that sticks to charcoal and recirculates through the bile. In enterohepatic recirculation, a drug goes to the liver, is moved into the bile, is put back into the small intestine, and then returns to the liver. A second or later dose of charcoal can catch the drug as it comes back into the gut. That is why multiple-dose charcoal can be used even after the toxin has been absorbed. In class she said a second dose of charcoal might catch some of the compound as it is re-injected into the small intestine through the bile, and that its use is limited to things that stick to charcoal and undergo that recirculation. A large volume of distribution is the problem for hemodialysis, not the indication for charcoal. A weak acid trapped in the urine is ion trapping with sodium bicarbonate, to enhance renal elimination. Timing, such as more than twenty-four hours, does not create the indication. Recirculation does.",
"L15-062": "The poison control centers have said these methods are risky and should be left to the emergency team's judgment. Each method has its own danger. Emesis and vomited charcoal risk aspiration. In lavage the tube can go into the lungs, and it is dangerous with an esophageal rupture. Whole bowel irrigation needs an open gut with no blockage. Weighing those risks, the American and European poison control centers have said in the last decade that the methods are dangerous and should be used only when really necessary. In class she said they all basically say they are dangerous and risky, use them only if you really have to, so it is up to the judgment of the people in the emergency room. She added that for a massive, dangerous ingestion, experienced toxicologists will probably still try gastric lavage and whole bowel irrigation, but probably not emesis. The statements do not recommend using the methods together, do not prefer emesis, and do not say charcoal has replaced the others.",
"L15-063": "The evidence comes from published case reports and case series, evaluated and graded by expert authors. Clinicians, pharmacists and doctors write up real overdoses and publish them. Experts in the field pulled every case report and case series, evaluated each one, and wrote the guidelines. The evidence and the recommendations are graded, as in other guidelines, so the documents are evidence based even without clinical trials. In class she said the data comes from people overdosing, with clinicians writing up the cases, and that the experts evaluated every single one of those case reports or case series. Randomized trials in volunteers are wrong, because no one can ethically be consented to overdose. Animal studies are wrong too. Animals are used very rarely, only to test whether a treatment really works. Manufacturer data from package inserts and labels is not the source either.",
"L15-064": "Evidence-based consensus guidelines for out-of-hospital management have been written for beta-blockers, calcium channel blockers and tricyclic antidepressants, among a short list. The full list is acetaminophen, atypical antipsychotics, beta-blockers, calcium channel blockers, dextromethorphan, diphenhydramine and dimenhydrinate, iron, methylphenidate, salicylate, selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors, tricyclic antidepressants, valproic acid, elemental mercury, ethylene glycol, and long-acting anticoagulant rodenticides. In class she said only a few drugs have these documents written, but these are pretty nasty drugs, and the two discussed in depth are beta-blockers and calcium channel blockers. Digoxin is not on the list. It comes up instead as the target of digoxin immune Fab, an ovine antibody fragment that inactivates the poison. Organophosphates are not on the list either. They come up under the pharmacological treatments, atropine and pralidoxime.",
"L15-065": "The guidelines contrast the two drug classes, and immediate-release versus extended-release tablets. Management differs slightly between beta-blocker and calcium channel blocker ingestions, even though both are cardiac drugs, and it differs by release form. Her key gives the windows for a patient who is asymptomatic when found. After an immediate-release product, the patient goes to a hospital if found within six hours, with sotalol the exception among beta-blockers. After a sustained-release beta-blocker the window is eight hours. After a modified-release calcium channel blocker other than verapamil it is eighteen hours, and after modified-release verapamil it is twenty-four hours. Found after the window and still asymptomatic, the patient can probably be managed at home with the poison center. The reason is the formulation. Immediate-release tablets are absorbed quickly. Extended-release products tend to form bezoars that may break apart at unpredictable times. Single- versus multiple-dose charcoal and emesis versus lavage are absorption choices, not contrasts these documents draw."
});
